var e=JSON.parse('[[1301,"The `bind`, `call` and `apply` methods let you change the context of a function\'s execution:\\n\\n- bind creates a new function with the context bound.\\n\\n- call calls the function immediately, passing it the context and the arguments.\\n\\n- apply is similar to `call`, but the arguments are passed in the form of an array.","In JavaScript the context of a function\'s execution is the object that the `this` keyword refers to. The `bind`, `call` and `apply` methods help you manage that context:\\n\\n- bind:\\n\\n- The `bind` method creates a new function, which on a call will always use the context given. This is convenient if you have to pass a function with a particular context.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n- call:\\n\\n- The `call` method calls a function immediately with the context and the arguments passed. This lets you call a function with an arbitrary context.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n- apply:\\n\\n- The `apply` method works analogously to `call`, but the arguments are passed in the form of an array.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe `bind`, `call` and `apply` methods matter for the management of the context in JavaScript. They let you call functions with the object you need, which is especially useful when working with asynchronous code and with events.","function greet() {\\n  console.log(`Hello, ${this.name}`);\\n}\\nconst person = { name: \'Alice\' };\\nconst greetPerson = greet.bind(person);\\ngreetPerson();  // \\"Hello, Alice\\"\\n\\nfunction greet(age) {\\n  console.log(`${this.name} is ${age} years old`);\\n}\\nconst person = { name: \'Bob\' };\\ngreet.call(person, 30);  // \\"Bob is 30 years old\\"\\n\\nfunction sum(a, b) {\\n  console.log(a + b);\\n}\\nsum.apply(null, [3, 4]);  // 7"],[1302,"The `reduce` function is used for the sequential handling of an array\'s elements with the preservation of an intermediate result. It takes a callback function, which receives two arguments — the accumulator and the current element of the array.","The `reduce` method lets you pass over every element of an array and reduce them to a single value. This method is useful for the aggregation of data — for counting a sum, finding a maximum or combining strings, for example.\\n\\n- The syntax:\\n\\n- `accumulator` is the value that preserves the intermediate results.\\n\\n- `currentValue` is the current element of the array.\\n\\n- `initialValue` is the initial value of the accumulator (optional).\\n\\n- An example with a sum:\\n\\n- An example with the search for the maximum value:\\n\\nConclusion:\\n`reduce` is a powerful tool for the aggregation of data in an array. It helps you gather information step by step, which is useful in the solving of tasks such as counting or the combining of values.","array.reduce((accumulator, currentValue) => {\\n  // an operation with accumulator and currentValue\\n}, initialValue);\\n\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];\\nconst sum = numbers.reduce((acc, current) => acc + current, 0);\\nconsole.log(sum);  // 10\\n\\nconst numbers = [10, 20, 30, 40];\\nconst max = numbers.reduce((max, current) => (current > max ? current : max), numbers[0]);\\nconsole.log(max);  // 40"],[1303,"To optimise the work with nested objects you can:\\n\\n- Use memoization to preserve the intermediate results.\\n\\n- Apply techniques of the lazy loading of data, in order to load only the parts that are necessary.\\n\\n- Use a `Map` or a `Set` for an efficient search.","Work with large nested objects can be expensive from the point of view of performance, especially if the data has to be changed or viewed often. Let us look at several approaches to the optimisation:\\n\\n- Memoization:\\n\\n- Memoization lets you save the results of computations and use them again, which reduces the time of the execution. It is applied in functions that are often called with the same parameters.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n- The lazy loading of data (Lazy Loading):\\n\\n- Data can be loaded as it is needed rather than loading the whole object at once. This is especially useful when working with data that may not always be needed.\\n\\n- The use of a `Map` and a `Set`:\\n\\n- When you have to work with large volumes of data and often search for or update elements, it is better to use data structures such as a `Map` or a `Set`, which ensure a fast search and insertion.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nTo optimise the work with large nested objects it matters to avoid unnecessary computations and to load only the data that is necessary. Memoization and lazy approaches help you raise the performance substantially.","const memoize = (fn) => {\\n  const cache = new Map();\\n  return (...args) => {\\n    const key = JSON.stringify(args);\\n    if (!cache.has(key)) {\\n      cache.set(key, fn(...args));\\n    }\\n    return cache.get(key);\\n  };\\n};"],[1304,"To improve the readability of code in loops you can use:\\n\\n- The decomposition of complex operations into separate functions.\\n\\n- The local use of variables for the intermediate results.\\n\\n- The use of array methods such as `map`, `filter` and `reduce` instead of ordinary loops.","When you work with large and complex data structures, loops can become difficult to take in. Here are several ways of improving the readability of the code:\\n\\n- Decomposition:\\n\\n- Split the complex operations carried out inside the loop into separate functions. This not only improves the readability but also helps you reuse the code.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n- The local use of variables:\\n\\n- Use comprehensible variables for holding the intermediate results, which helps you avoid confusion and makes the code more readable.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n- The use of array methods:\\n\\n- Instead of the standard loops you can use array methods such as `map`, `filter` and `reduce`, which make the code more declarative and more readable.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nTo raise the readability of code in loops it matters to use the principles of decomposition and comprehensible names of variables, and to apply the built-in array methods in order to make the code more comprehensible and more maintainable.","// The bad variant\\nfor (let i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {\\n  if (data[i].isActive) {\\n    processItem(data[i]);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The good variant\\nconst processActiveItems = (item) => {\\n  if (item.isActive) {\\n    processItem(item);\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nfor (let i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {\\n  processActiveItems(data[i]);\\n}\\n\\n// The local use of a variable for holding the intermediate values\\nconst result = processData(item);\\nif (result.isValid) {\\n  doSomething(result);\\n}\\n\\nconst activeItems = data.filter(item => item.isActive);\\nactiveItems.forEach(item => processItem(item));"],[1305,"To convert JSON into CSV you can use the following steps:\\n\\n- Convert the JSON into an array of objects.\\n\\n- Extract the objects\' keys as the headers.\\n\\n- Convert the data into a CSV string separated by commas.","JSON and CSV are two popular formats for the representation of data. To convert data from JSON into CSV, several steps have to be carried out:\\n\\n- The conversion of the JSON into an array of objects:\\n\\n- JSON is often represented as an array of objects. It matters to parse that array correctly and work with it.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n- The extraction of the objects\' keys for the CSV headers:\\n\\n- The objects\' keys become the headers of the columns in the CSV.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n- The conversion of the data into a CSV string:\\n\\n- For every object in the JSON a CSV row can be created, separating the values with commas.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n- The result:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe conversion of JSON into CSV is a process including the extraction of the data and its formatting into rows with separators. It matters to work with the headers and the values correctly in order to obtain the right result.","const jsonData = [\\n  { \\"name\\": \\"Alice\\", \\"age\\": 25 },\\n  { \\"name\\": \\"Bob\\", \\"age\\": 30 }\\n];\\n\\nconst headers = Object.keys(jsonData[0]);\\n\\nconst csvRows = [];\\ncsvRows.push(headers.join(\',\')); // We add the headers\\n\\njsonData.forEach(row => {\\n  csvRows.push(headers.map(header => row[header]).join(\',\'));\\n});\\n\\nconst csvString = csvRows.join(\'\\\\n\');\\nconsole.log(csvString);\\n\\nname,age\\nAlice,25\\nBob,30"],[1306,"The `Object.keys` and `Object.values` methods let you extract an object\'s keys and values respectively:\\n\\n- Object.keys returns an array of all of the object\'s keys.\\n\\n- Object.values returns an array of all of the object\'s values.","The `Object.keys` and `Object.values` methods are useful when working with objects, since they let you easily obtain arrays of the keys or of the values. Both methods are built into JavaScript and do not change the object itself.\\n\\n- Object.keys:\\n\\n- This method returns an array containing all of the object\'s keys. The array includes only the object\'s enumerable properties.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n- Object.values:\\n\\n- This method returns an array containing all of the object\'s values for the corresponding keys.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n- A comparison with the other methods:\\n\\n- Besides `keys` and `values`, there is also the `Object.entries` method, which returns an array of [key, value] pairs.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe `Object.keys` and `Object.values` methods are useful for obtaining information about an object\'s structure in the form of an array. These methods make work with objects in JavaScript more convenient and more readable.","const person = { name: \'Alice\', age: 25 };\\nconst keys = Object.keys(person);\\nconsole.log(keys);  // [\'name\', \'age\']\\n\\nconst person = { name: \'Alice\', age: 25 };\\nconst values = Object.values(person);\\nconsole.log(values);  // [\'Alice\', 25]\\n\\nconst entries = Object.entries(person);\\nconsole.log(entries);  // [[\'name\', \'Alice\'], [\'age\', 25]]"],[1307,"For work with Canvas and 2D graphics both the native Canvas API and wrapper libraries are used. The popular solutions simplify the work with shapes, events, transformations and the state of the scene. The choice depends on the complexity of the scene and on the requirements of interactivity and performance. For simple tasks pure Canvas is enough; for complex ones libraries are more convenient.","Work with 2D graphics in a browser can be carried out at different levels of abstraction. Before choosing a tool it matters to understand what task it solves.\\nThe native approach\\nThe Canvas API is a low-level interface for the drawing of pixels.\\n\\n- Complete control over the painting\\n\\n- Maximum performance\\n\\n- All of the logic of the scene and of the states is implemented by hand\\n\\nIt is used when:\\n\\n- custom rendering is required\\n\\n- high performance matters\\n\\n- the scene is relatively simple in its logic\\n\\nLibraries on top of Canvas\\nThese solutions take on the management of the scene, the objects and the events.\\n\\n- Fabric.js\\n\\n- An object model (shapes, groups, transformations)\\n\\n- Convenient work with SVG\\n\\n- It is suitable for editors and configurators\\n\\n- Konva.js\\n\\n- Layers and scenes\\n\\n- Good integration with React\\n\\n- It is often used for interactive interfaces\\n\\n- PixiJS\\n\\n- High performance\\n\\n- It is often applied in games\\n\\n- It uses WebGL, but it is suitable for 2D too\\n\\n- Paper.js\\n\\n- Vector graphics\\n\\n- It is convenient for illustrations and geometry\\n\\n- It is less oriented towards UI scenarios\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nFor UI-oriented and interactive scenes Fabric.js or Konva.js are more often chosen. For games and complex animation, PixiJS. Pure Canvas is justified when maximum flexibility and control are needed.",null],[1308,"SVG and Canvas use different models of rendering. SVG is declarative and object-based, Canvas is imperative and pixel-based. In the painting of SVG into a Canvas its structure and its interactivity are lost. SVG is therefore usually first parsed and then \\"repainted\\" into the Canvas.","SVG and Canvas differ fundamentally in their architecture, and that directly affects the way they are used together.\\nThe difference of the models of rendering\\nSVG:\\n\\n- DOM-oriented\\n\\n- Every element is a separate node\\n\\n- It preserves the structure and the hierarchy\\n\\nCanvas:\\n\\n- A pixel buffer\\n\\n- After the painting there is no information about the objects\\n\\n- There is no built-in structure of the scene\\n\\nWhat happens in the painting of SVG into a Canvas\\nThe process usually looks like this:\\n\\n- The SVG is loaded as a file or a string\\n\\n- It is parsed by the browser or by a library\\n\\n- The SVG elements are converted into Canvas commands\\n\\nAs a result:\\n\\n- The SVG becomes a \\"flat\\" image\\n\\n- The groups, ids and the semantics disappear\\n\\n- The SVG\'s events and styles no longer work automatically\\n\\nThe practical consequences\\n\\n- Events cannot be hung directly on the SVG elements\\n\\n- Any transformations have to be tracked by hand\\n\\n- A change of one object requires the complete or the partial repainting of the scene\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nCanvas cannot work with SVG as with structured objects. In the painting SVG turns into a set of pixels, so all of the logic of the interaction and of the state has to be implemented separately.",null],[1309,"Fabric.js on the import of SVG converts the elements into Canvas objects. The groups of an SVG can be combined into a `Group`, but the hierarchy is often simplified. There are no layers in the classical sense; the order of the objects is determined by their position in an array. The management of the groups and of the order of the painting is carried out programmatically.","Fabric.js uses an object model of its own on top of Canvas, which corresponds only partially to the structure of SVG.\\nThe import of SVG into Fabric.js\\nOn the loading of an SVG:\\n\\n- Every SVG element turns into a Fabric object\\n\\n- A `<g>` may be converted into a `fabric.Group`\\n\\n- Part of the nesting may be lost or simplified\\n\\nThis is connected with the fact that Fabric is oriented towards Canvas rendering rather than towards a DOM hierarchy.\\nWork with groups\\nA `Group` in Fabric.js:\\n\\n- Combines several objects\\n\\n- Applies transformations to the whole group\\n\\n- Can be broken back up into separate elements\\n\\nImportant:\\n\\n- Deep nesting of an SVG is often \\"flattened\\"\\n\\n- Not every SVG attribute has a direct analogue\\n\\nLayers in Fabric.js\\nIn Fabric.js there are no layers as a separate entity.\\nWhat is used:\\n\\n- The order of the objects in `canvas._objects`\\n\\n- The methods `bringToFront` and `sendToBack`\\n\\n- Logical grouping through a `Group`\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nFabric.js supports groups but does not preserve the SVG hierarchy completely. Layers are implemented through the order of the objects, so complex SVG structures require additional logic in the import and in the management of the scene.",null],[1310,"If a library \\"flattens\\" an SVG, the hierarchy can be restored at the stage of the parsing. For that the original SVG DOM or the elements\' metadata is used. The groups are gathered by hand on the basis of the `id`, the `data-*` attributes or the nesting. That structure is then mapped onto the Canvas objects.","Many Canvas libraries simplify the SVG structure, since they do not need the complete DOM hierarchy. In complex editors, however, the hierarchy of the groups is often critical.\\nThe main idea of the restoration\\nThe restoration is built not on the Canvas but on the data:\\n\\n- The SVG contains the complete structure\\n\\n- The Canvas has only the result of the painting\\n\\nYou therefore first have to preserve or restore the structure, and only then work with the Canvas objects.\\nThe practical approaches\\n\\n- The parsing of the SVG before it is passed to the library\\n\\n- `DOMParser` is used\\n\\n- A pass over the `<g>`s and the child elements\\n\\n- The forming of a tree of your own\\n\\n- The use of metadata\\n\\n- `id`\\n\\n- `data-group`\\n\\n- custom attributes\\n\\n- The mapping onto the Canvas objects\\n\\n- Every Canvas object is assigned a reference to a node of the structure\\n\\n- The groups are gathered by hand through a `Group`\\n\\nAn example of the logic (simplified)\\n\\nThe limitations of the approach\\n\\n- Additional memory is required\\n\\n- It is harder to synchronise the changes\\n\\n- The library may not support nested groups directly\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nThe hierarchy of an SVG is restored not by the means of Canvas but through the preliminary analysis of the SVG structure. This requires a data model of your own, but it gives complete control over the groups and the layers.","// svgNode -> the list of child elements\\n// then we create a fabric.Group on the basis of those connections"],[1311,"Canvas does not hold the state of the objects, so it cannot be \\"rolled back\\" automatically. For Undo/Redo you have to save the history of the changes yourself. The main difficulties are connected with the volume of the data, the performance and the correct restoration of the scene. Often you have to choose between the holding of snapshots of the state and the holding of operations.","Canvas is an imperative API, and after the painting it does not know what objects and actions led to it. That directly affects the implementation of Undo/Redo.\\nThe key difficulties\\nBefore the implementation it matters to understand the main problems:\\n\\n- The absence of built-in state\\n\\n- Canvas knows nothing of the shapes and the layers\\n\\n- All of the state has to be held separately\\n\\n- The volume of the data\\n\\n- Complete snapshots of the scene can be large\\n\\n- Frequent changes quickly inflate the history\\n\\n- Performance\\n\\n- The restoration of the state may require a complete repainting\\n\\n- That is especially critical for complex scenes\\n\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- The holding of snapshots of the state\\n\\n- the serialisation of every object\\n\\n- a simple rollback\\n\\n- a large expenditure of memory\\n\\n- The holding of commands\\n\\n- the actions are saved (add, move, resize)\\n\\n- less memory\\n\\n- it is harder to implement\\n\\nThe typical flow of Undo/Redo\\n\\n- an action -> a record in the history\\n\\n- Undo -> the rollback of the state\\n\\n- Redo -> the repeat of the action\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nUndo/Redo for Canvas is a task of the management of the history of the scene\'s state. The choice between snapshots and commands depends on the complexity of the scene and on the requirements of performance.",null],[1312,"Graphics libraries are extended through their public APIs, plugins or inheritance. In extreme cases wrappers or patches are used. It matters to avoid the direct change of a library\'s source code. The customisation has to be isolated and updatable.","Third-party libraries rarely fit a specific product ideally, so they have to be extended.\\nThe main ways of extending\\n\\n- The use of the public API\\n\\n- events\\n\\n- hooks\\n\\n- configuration options\\n\\n- Inheritance and the extension of classes\\n\\n- the addition of new behaviour\\n\\n- the overriding of methods\\n\\n- Wrappers\\n\\n- the library is used as an \\"engine\\"\\n\\n- the external logic lives separately\\n\\n- Plugins and middleware\\n\\n- if the library supports that approach\\n\\nWhat is worth avoiding\\n\\n- edits of the code in `node_modules`\\n\\n- a dependence on private methods\\n\\n- logic rigidly tied to a version of the library\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nSafe customisation is built around public APIs and the isolation of the changes. The less the library \\"breaks\\" on an update, the better the architectural solution.","class CustomShape extends BaseShape {\\n  // we add additional logic\\n}"],[1313,"Canvas 2D is a simple API for the painting of 2D graphics. WebGL is a low-level interface for work with the GPU and 3D scenes. Canvas is simpler to master, WebGL is more powerful and more performant. The choice depends on the complexity of the graphics and on the requirements of the animation.","Canvas 2D and WebGL solve different classes of task, despite their outward similarity.\\nCanvas 2D\\nCanvas 2D is suitable for:\\n\\n- simple 2D scenes\\n\\n- UI graphics\\n\\n- diagrams and editors\\n\\nThe characteristics:\\n\\n- an imperative API\\n\\n- CPU rendering\\n\\n- a simple model of thinking\\n\\nWebGL / 3D\\nWebGL is oriented towards:\\n\\n- complex animation\\n\\n- large scenes\\n\\n- 3D graphics\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- work directly with the GPU\\n\\n- shaders\\n\\n- a higher barrier to entry\\n\\nThe practical consequences of the choice\\n\\n- Canvas is simpler to maintain\\n\\n- WebGL scales better in performance\\n\\n- For most UI tasks WebGL is excessive\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nCanvas 2D is the choice for simple and medium 2D tasks. WebGL is justified when high performance, complex animation or 3D scenes are needed.",null],[1314,"Three.js can be used to add 3D effects to 2D graphics. It lets you apply hardware acceleration, complex transformations and animations. Three.js often acts as the render engine, while the 2D graphics is laid over it or used as textures. Such an approach is applied for visual effects and interactive scenes.","Although Three.js is associated with 3D, it is often used in hybrid 2D scenarios too.\\nThe main scenarios of use\\nBefore the application it matters to understand why Three.js should be attached at all:\\n\\n- the need for GPU acceleration\\n\\n- complex animations and effects\\n\\n- work with perspective and depth\\n\\nThe typical variants of integration\\n\\n- 2D as textures\\n\\n- A Canvas or an SVG is rendered into a texture\\n\\n- the texture is applied to a plane (`PlaneGeometry`)\\n\\n- Pseudo-3D effects\\n\\n- parallax\\n\\n- tilts and rotations\\n\\n- depth-based animations\\n\\n- Complex visual effects\\n\\n- blur\\n\\n- glow\\n\\n- post-processing\\n\\nThe limitations of the approach\\n\\n- a higher complexity of maintenance\\n\\n- more code and abstractions\\n\\n- it is not always justified for simple UI tasks\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nThree.js in combination with 2D graphics is applied when a standard Canvas cannot cope with the visual requirements. This is justified for effects and performant scenes, but it is excessive for an ordinary UI.","// A Canvas is used as a texture for Three.js\\nconst texture = new THREE.CanvasTexture(canvas)"],[1315,"Event handlers belong to reactive and event-oriented tasks. They are not carried out constantly but fire in response to the actions of the user or of the system. Such tasks manage the flow of the interaction rather than computations. This is a key part of user interfaces.","Event handlers are neither business logic nor computational tasks in the pure sense.\\nThe nature of the task\\nEvent listeners belong to:\\n\\n- event-oriented programming\\n\\n- reactive systems\\n\\n- the management of the user\'s interaction\\n\\nThey \\"react\\" rather than initiating a process.\\nWhat event handlers do\\n\\n- They receive an event\\n\\n- a click\\n\\n- the typing of text\\n\\n- a network response\\n\\n- They start a reaction\\n\\n- the update of state\\n\\n- the call of business logic\\n\\n- the repainting of the UI\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- events set the interface\'s lifecycle\\n\\n- the logic becomes asynchronous\\n\\n- the code has to be robust to frequent calls\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nEvent handlers are reactive tasks governing an application\'s flow. They connect the user\'s actions and changes of state, but they should contain no heavy logic.",null],[1316,"`await` inside a condition works just as it does in any other place. The execution of the function is suspended until the Promise has finished. The condition is evaluated only after the result has been obtained. This can affect the performance and the readability of the code.","`await` does not change the logic of conditional constructs, but it affects the order of the execution.\\nHow `await` works in a condition\\nWhen `await` is used:\\n\\n- the execution of the current async function is suspended\\n\\n- the event loop carries on working\\n\\n- after the Promise has finished the code continues\\n\\nThe practical consequences\\n\\n- The condition becomes asynchronous\\n\\n- the result depends on the Promise\\n\\n- a delay in the execution is possible\\n\\n- Sequential execution\\n\\n- several `await`s in conditions are carried out in turn\\n\\n- The potential problems\\n\\n- superfluous waits\\n\\n- the difficulty of reading the logic\\n\\nWhen this is justified\\n\\n- the checking of the rights of access\\n\\n- the loading of configuration\\n\\n- validation before an action\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\n`await` inside conditions is safe and predictable, but it makes the flow of the execution asynchronous. It is worth using knowingly, so as not to complicate and not to slow the code down.","if (await isAllowed()) {\\n  // it will be carried out only after the Promise has finished\\n}"],[1317,"`setTimeout` places the callback in the queue of macrotasks. The Promise inside it is created and resolved during the execution of that macrotask. The Promise\'s microtasks will be carried out right after the current `setTimeout` callback has finished, but before the next macrotask. This affects the order of the execution of the code.","Asynchronicity in JavaScript is founded on queues of tasks, and `setTimeout` with a `Promise` involve different levels of that system.\\nThe queues of tasks in the browser\\nBefore the analysis of the example it matters to understand:\\n\\n- `setTimeout` -> a macrotask\\n\\n- `Promise.then` / `await` -> a microtask\\n\\nMicrotasks are always carried out before the next macrotask.\\nThe order of the execution\\n\\nThe sequence:\\n\\n- The synchronous code is carried out\\n\\n- The `setTimeout` callback lands in the queue of macrotasks\\n\\n- After its execution the Promise\'s microtask is added\\n\\n- The microtask is carried out before the next macrotask\\n\\nThe practical consequences\\n\\n- A Promise inside a `setTimeout` does not \\"overtake\\" the synchronous code\\n\\n- But it is carried out before the other timers\\n\\n- This matters in the optimisation and the debugging of asynchronous code\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nA Promise created inside a `setTimeout` is carried out within the bounds of the timer\'s macrotask, and its handlers as microtasks right after it. An understanding of this order is critical for predictable asynchronous behaviour.","setTimeout(() => {\\n  Promise.resolve().then(() => {\\n    console.log(\'promise\')\\n  })\\n}, 0)\\n\\nconsole.log(\'sync\')"],[1318,"Debounce postpones the execution of a function until the events have ceased for a given time. This lowers the number of updates of state and of re-renders during the typing of text. Debounce is usually applied for search, filtering and requests to an API. As a result the interface works more smoothly and there are fewer requests.","Typing into an `input` generates events very often: practically on every character. If heavy work (a render, filtering, a request) is done on every event, the application will begin to lag.\\nWhat debouncing does\\nDebounce is the strategy of \\"carry it out later, when the user has finished\\".\\n\\n- the user types -> the timer is constantly reset\\n\\n- the user has stopped -> the timer has expired -> the function has been carried out\\n\\nThis differs from throttle, where the execution takes place \\"no more often than once in N ms\\".\\nThe typical cases of use\\n\\n- A search as text is typed\\n\\n- not to make a request on every character\\n\\n- The filtering of a large list\\n\\n- not to recompute the filter on every keystroke\\n\\n- The autosaving of a draft\\n\\n- to save after a pause\\n\\nAn example of debounce without libraries\\n\\nThe application in React usually looks like a separation:\\n\\n- `inputValue` is updated at once (so that the UI is responsive)\\n\\n- \\"debouncedValue\\" is updated more rarely (for the heavy actions)\\n\\nAn important point about state and UX\\nIf you do the debounce right on the `setState` of the input field, you can get a bad UX:\\n\\n- the field \\"lags behind\\" the typing\\n\\n- the cursor behaves strangely\\n\\n- it seems as though the application is lagging\\n\\nMore often it is more correct:\\n\\n- to display the input instantaneously\\n\\n- to debounce the effect (the request or the filtering) rather than the input itself\\n\\nThe typical mistakes\\n\\n- The creation of the debounce function on every render\\n\\n- then the timers will be reset unpredictably\\n\\n- the solution: stabilise the function through `useMemo` / `useCallback`\\n\\n- Forgetting to clear the timer on unmount\\n\\n- it can lead to an update of state after the unmounting\\n\\n- the solution: a cleanup in the `useEffect`\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nDebounce is needed in order to reduce the number of heavy operations on frequent events and to make the UI smoother. In React the value of the input is usually updated at once, and the debounce is applied to the side actions: filtering, requests, autosaving.","function debounce(fn, delay) {\\n  let t\\n  return (...args) => {\\n    clearTimeout(t)\\n    t = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), delay)\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst [value, setValue] = useState(\'\')\\n\\nconst onChange = (e) => setValue(e.target.value)\\n\\n// then: the debounce either for the request or for the computations"],[1319,"Virtualisation without libraries is implemented through the computation of the visible area and the render of only the elements you need. A container with scrolling is used, and the range of the indexes that have to be shown is computed. The rest of the space is compensated for by padding. Such an approach requires careful computations and optimisation.","Hand-written virtualisation is a manageable compromise between complexity and control.\\nThe basic idea\\nYou have to answer three questions:\\n\\n- which part of the list is visible\\n\\n- which elements land there\\n\\n- where the content has to begin and end\\n\\nThe minimal algorithm\\n\\n- Known:\\n\\n- the height of the container\\n\\n- the height of one element\\n\\n- From the `scrollTop` the following are computed:\\n\\n- `startIndex`\\n\\n- `endIndex`\\n\\n- The elements are rendered:\\n\\n- only in the range `[startIndex, endIndex]`\\n\\n- The compensation of the scrolling:\\n\\n- `padding-top` = `startIndex * itemHeight`\\n\\n- `padding-bottom` = `(total - endIndex) * itemHeight`\\n\\nA simplified example of the logic\\n\\nThe main difficulties\\n\\n- different heights of the elements\\n\\n- the smoothness of the scrolling\\n\\n- frequent recomputations on scroll\\n\\n- support for resize\\n\\nBecause of those difficulties libraries remain popular.\\nWhen a hand-written implementation is justified\\n\\n- very specific requirements\\n\\n- a minimal bundle\\n\\n- complete control over the logic\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nVirtualisation without libraries is built on the computation of the visible area and the management of the padding. This is possible, but it requires a careful implementation and an understanding of the browser\'s internal mechanisms.","const start = Math.floor(scrollTop / itemHeight)\\nconst visibleCount = Math.ceil(containerHeight / itemHeight)\\nconst end = start + visibleCount"],[1320,"The main API for tracking the visibility of elements is the `IntersectionObserver`. It lets you find out asynchronously when an element appears in or disappears from the viewport. In simpler cases scroll events and `getBoundingClientRect` are used, but that is less efficient. Modern applications almost always choose the `IntersectionObserver`.","The tracking of the visibility of elements matters for lazy loading, virtualisation and analytics.\\nThe main API: IntersectionObserver\\nThe `IntersectionObserver` reports:\\n\\n- when an element enters the area of visibility\\n\\n- how far it is visible (the intersection ratio)\\n\\n- relative to which container the visibility is counted\\n\\nThe key particulars:\\n\\n- it works asynchronously\\n\\n- it requires no manual handling of scroll events\\n\\n- it is optimised at the level of the browser\\n\\nThe alternative approaches\\n\\n- Scroll + getBoundingClientRect\\n\\n- the manual computation of the coordinates\\n\\n- a high frequency of calls\\n\\n- the risk of problems with performance\\n\\n- ResizeObserver (indirectly)\\n\\n- it tracks the change of dimensions\\n\\n- it is used together with an IntersectionObserver\\n\\n- The Visibility API\\n\\n- it determines whether the tab is active\\n\\n- it is not about elements but about the page as a whole\\n\\nWhen to use which\\n\\n- the lazy loading of images -> `IntersectionObserver`\\n\\n- the analytics of the viewing of blocks -> `IntersectionObserver`\\n\\n- simple scenarios -> `getBoundingClientRect` (with care)\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nThe `IntersectionObserver` is the standard and most efficient way of tracking the visibility of elements. Manual solutions through scroll are suitable only for simple or specific cases.","const observer = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {\\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\\n    if (entry.isIntersecting) {\\n      // the element is visible\\n    }\\n  })\\n})\\n\\nobserver.observe(element)"],[1321,"The search for anagrams is implemented by means of a sliding window and frequency tables of the characters. First the frequency of the characters of the substring sought is counted, then a window of the same length \\"slides\\" along the string. On a coincidence of the frequencies an occurrence has been found. Such an algorithm works in linear time.","The task of the search for anagrams is a classic example of the application of the sliding window technique.\\nWhat an anagram is in this task\\nAn anagram is:\\n\\n- the same characters\\n\\n- in any order\\n\\n- with the same number of repetitions\\n\\nThe order of the characters does not matter; what matters is the frequency.\\nThe naive approach and its problem\\nThe naive solution:\\n\\n- take every substring\\n\\n- sort the characters\\n\\n- compare with the sorted pattern\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- a sort at every step\\n\\n- a complexity higher than linear\\n\\nThe efficient approach: a sliding window\\nThe idea:\\n\\n- Build a frequency map of the substring `pattern`\\n\\n- Keep a frequency map of the current window in the string\\n\\n- Shift the window by one character\\n\\n- Update the frequencies without recomputing everything anew\\n\\nAn example of the basic logic\\n\\nImportant:\\n\\n- the length of the window is always equal to the length of the substring\\n\\n- we add the character on the right\\n\\n- we remove the character on the left\\n\\nWhy this is efficient\\n\\n- every character is added and removed once\\n\\n- there are no nested loops\\n\\n- the complexity is O(n)\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nThe search for the anagrams of a substring in a string is efficiently solved through a sliding window and frequency maps. This lets you avoid brute force and sorting.","// the frequencies of the pattern and of the current window are compared\\n// if they coincide, an occurrence has been found"],[1322,"To find all of the indexes the same algorithm with a sliding window is used. On every coincidence of the frequencies of the current window with the pattern the index of the beginning of the window is saved. As a result an array of all of the positions is returned. The algorithm remains linear in time.","The search for the indexes is a logical extension of the task of the search for the anagrams themselves.\\nThe basic idea\\nThe same sliding window is used, but in addition:\\n\\n- the current position of the window is tracked\\n\\n- on a coincidence of the frequencies the index is saved\\n\\nThe sequence of the actions\\n\\n- The preparation\\n\\n- the frequency map of the substring\\n\\n- an empty array of results\\n\\n- The initialisation of the window\\n\\n- the first `k` characters of the string\\n\\n- The shift of the window\\n\\n- the addition of a new character\\n\\n- the removal of the old one\\n\\n- The check of the coincidence\\n\\n- if the maps are equal -> `push(startIndex)`\\n\\nA simplified example of the structure\\n\\nWhat it matters to pay attention to\\n\\n- the correct update of the frequencies on the shift\\n\\n- an identical size of the window\\n\\n- careful work with the characters whose frequency has become zero\\n\\nThe possible optimisations\\n\\n- the use of arrays instead of objects for a limited alphabet\\n\\n- a counter of the characters that have coincided instead of the complete comparison of the maps\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nAll of the indexes of anagrams are found by the same sliding window algorithm as the single occurrences. The difference is only in the accumulation of the results, while the asymptotics remains linear.","const result = []\\n\\n// on a coincidence:\\nresult.push(startIndex)"],[1323,"The main approach to the comparison of anagrams is the comparison of the frequencies of the characters. If the frequencies of all of the characters coincide, the strings are anagrams. The sorting of the strings is possible but less efficient. For tasks with large volumes of data the approach with frequency tables and linear complexity is preferable.","Anagram-hood is determined not by the order of the characters but by their quantity. From that several possible approaches follow, each with its own pluses and minuses.\\nThe naive approach: the sorting of the strings\\nThe idea is simple:\\n\\n- convert both strings into an array of characters\\n\\n- sort them\\n\\n- compare the result\\n\\nThe problems of the approach:\\n\\n- the sort has a complexity of O(n log n)\\n\\n- superfluous allocations of memory\\n\\n- it scales badly for frequent checks\\n\\nIt is used mainly:\\n\\n- in teaching examples\\n\\n- with very short strings\\n\\nThe main approach: frequency tables\\nThe more correct and more scalable strategy is to count the frequencies of the characters.\\nThe general idea:\\n\\n- for the first string, increment the counters of the characters\\n\\n- for the second string, decrement them\\n\\n- at the end every counter has to be equal to zero\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- linear complexity O(n)\\n\\n- it does not depend on the order of the characters\\n\\n- it is well suited for a sliding window\\n\\nThe optimisations for a limited alphabet\\nIf the alphabet is known and limited (only `a–z`, for example):\\n\\n- an array of a fixed length can be used\\n\\n- that is faster and more economical in memory\\n\\nThe approach with a counter of coincidences\\nFor the tasks of the search for anagrams in a string what is often used is:\\n\\n- a common counter of the characters that have coincided\\n\\n- its decrement or increment on the shift of the window\\n\\nThis lets you:\\n\\n- not compare the tables in their entirety\\n\\n- obtain strictly linear time\\n\\nWhat it matters to take into account in practice\\n\\n- The length of the strings\\n\\n- a different length -> not anagrams, at once\\n\\n- The set of characters\\n\\n- the case\\n\\n- spaces\\n\\n- special characters\\n\\n- The frequency of the checks\\n\\n- a single check\\n\\n- a mass search in a string\\n\\nWhich approach to choose\\n\\n- a single check, short strings -> a sort is permissible\\n\\n- frequent checks or a search in a text -> frequency tables\\n\\n- large volumes of data -> arrays + a sliding window\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nIn practice anagrams are compared through the frequencies of the characters rather than by sorting. This gives linear complexity, scales well and is easily applied in the tasks of the search and the scanning of strings.","const isAnagram = (a, b) =>\\n  a.split(\'\').sort().join(\'\') === b.split(\'\').sort().join(\'\')\\n\\nfunction isAnagram(a, b) {\\n  if (a.length !== b.length) return false\\n\\n  const map = {}\\n\\n  for (const ch of a) map[ch] = (map[ch] || 0) + 1\\n  for (const ch of b) {\\n    if (!map[ch]) return false\\n    map[ch]--\\n  }\\n\\n  return true\\n}\\n\\nconst freq = new Array(26).fill(0)"],[1324,"Intermittent bugs first have to be reproduced stably. It matters to gather the context: the environment, the user\'s actions, the application\'s state. The debugging is conducted through logs, the isolation of the code and the simplification of the scenario. The hypotheses are checked one at a time. Without a reproduction a bug is not considered fixed.","Unstable bugs are among the most complex, since they depend on timing, on state or on the environment.\\nThe first step is the reproduction\\nWithout a reproduction the debugging turns into guesswork.\\n\\n- the recording of the user\'s steps\\n\\n- the checking of different environments\\n\\n- the analysis of the frequency of the appearance\\n\\nThe gathering of the context\\nIt matters to understand under what conditions the error arises:\\n\\n- The application\'s state\\n\\n- the data\\n\\n- the authorisation\\n\\n- the flags\\n\\n- Asynchronicity\\n\\n- races of requests\\n\\n- unsynchronised updates\\n\\n- The environment\\n\\n- the browser\\n\\n- the device\\n\\n- the network\\n\\nThe approach to the search for the cause\\n\\n- the addition of logs at the key points\\n\\n- the simplification of the scenario to the minimum\\n\\n- the temporary disabling of parts of the logic\\n\\n- the checking of the hypotheses in turn\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe frequent causes of intermittent bugs\\n\\n- a race condition\\n\\n- stale state\\n\\n- errors in the dependencies in effects\\n\\n- the mutation of data\\n\\nConclusion\\nIntermittent bugs require discipline and a systematic approach. The main goal is to turn an unstable error into a reproducible one, and only then to eliminate its cause.","console.log(\'state before update\', state)"],[1325,"Work with unfamiliar code has to begin with a general understanding of the task that it solves. Then the point of entry and the main dependencies are studied. It matters not to bring in changes at once but first to get to grips with the code\'s behaviour. It is useful to use logs and the debugger. Any edits have to be minimal and verifiable.","Work with unfamiliar JavaScript code is a regular task in the maintenance and the development of existing projects.\\nThe initial stage is orientation in the code\\nBefore the analysis of the details it matters to obtain a general impression:\\n\\n- what business task the code solves\\n\\n- where the point of entry is\\n\\n- what modules and layers are involved\\n\\nThe sequence of the study\\n\\n- Reading \\"from the top down\\"\\n\\n- the entry file\\n\\n- the main functions or components\\n\\n- The search for the key entities\\n\\n- the state\\n\\n- the side effects\\n\\n- the work with the API\\n\\n- The analysis of the data\\n\\n- where the data comes from\\n\\n- how it is transformed\\n\\n- where it is passed on to\\n\\nThe practical tools\\n\\n- the browser\'s debugger\\n\\n- `console.log` at the control points\\n\\n- the temporary simplification of the code for the checking of hypotheses\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe rules of safe changes\\n\\n- do not rewrite code without understanding\\n\\n- change one thing at a time\\n\\n- check the behaviour at once\\n\\nConclusion\\nWork with unfamiliar code is a process of investigation. Haste and chaotic edits almost always lead to new errors.","console.log(\'current value\', value)"],[1326,"TDD is an approach in which the tests are written before the code. First the expected behaviour is described, then the minimal code is implemented. After that the code is improved without a change in the behaviour. Such an approach helps you think the architecture through better. TDD lowers the quantity of errors.","TDD is built around the idea that a system\'s behaviour should be described before its implementation.\\nThe classic cycle of TDD\\n\\n- Red\\n\\n- a test is written\\n\\n- the test fails\\n\\n- Green\\n\\n- the minimal code is written\\n\\n- the test passes\\n\\n- Refactor\\n\\n- the code is simplified\\n\\n- the behaviour is preserved\\n\\nWhat TDD gives in practice\\n\\n- a clear understanding of the requirements\\n\\n- more modular code\\n\\n- high testability\\n\\nWhen TDD is especially useful\\n\\n- complex business logic\\n\\n- libraries and utilities\\n\\n- code with a long lifespan\\n\\nThe limitations of the approach\\n\\n- it requires discipline\\n\\n- it increases the time at the start\\n\\nConclusion\\nTDD is not about tests but about a way of thinking and of designing code through behaviour.",null],[1327,"TDD requires high discipline and experience. At the start the development slows down because of the need to write the tests in advance. It is not always obvious how to test a UI and asynchronous behaviour. With frequent changes of the requirements the tests have to be constantly rewritten. Without an understanding of the goal TDD turns into a formality.","Despite its advantages, TDD is not a universal solution and it has a number of practical difficulties.\\nThe main problems of TDD\\n\\n- A raised barrier to entry\\n\\n- it is hard to begin without experience\\n\\n- it is difficult to write the tests before the code\\n\\n- A slowdown at the early stages\\n\\n- more time goes on the start\\n\\n- the result is not visible at once\\n\\n- The non-obviousness of the tests for a UI\\n\\n- it is hard to describe the behaviour in advance\\n\\n- there are many changes at the stage of the design\\n\\n- Frequent edits of the tests\\n\\n- the requirements change\\n\\n- the tests quickly become outdated\\n\\nThe typical mistakes in the use\\n\\n- the writing of tests for the sake of tests\\n\\n- tests that are too detailed\\n\\n- the testing of the implementation instead of the behaviour\\n\\nWhen TDD is not worth applying\\n\\n- prototypes and MVPs\\n\\n- tasks with a high uncertainty\\n\\n- a fast-changing UI\\n\\nConclusion\\nTDD is effective with correct application and stable requirements. Otherwise it can slow the development down and increase the cost of changes.",null],[1328,"jQuery simplified the work with the DOM and with cross-browser compatibility at the time when the native API was inconvenient and inconsistent. It offered a short and uniform syntax for the search for elements, the handling of events and the work with AJAX. Today, however, the native DOM API has become substantially more convenient and more powerful. jQuery adds a superfluous layer of abstraction and weight to the bundle. In modern applications it is used ever more rarely.","jQuery appeared as a solution to the problems of the early web, when browsers differed greatly in their behaviour and their capabilities.\\nThe advantages of jQuery\\nThe main strengths of jQuery are connected with the simplification of the development:\\n\\n- a single API for different browsers\\n\\n- a short and readable syntax for work with the DOM\\n\\n- built-in utilities for events, animations and AJAX\\n\\n- a low barrier to entry for beginners\\n\\nAn example of the selection of an element:\\n\\nThe disadvantages of jQuery\\nOver time its limitations became obvious:\\n\\n- the additional weight of the library in the bundle\\n\\n- its redundancy when there is a modern DOM API\\n\\n- the difficulty of integration with component frameworks\\n\\n- an outdated model of work with the interface\'s state\\n\\nThe modern native DOM API\\nToday browsers provide:\\n\\n- `querySelector` and `querySelectorAll`\\n\\n- convenient work with classes through `classList`\\n\\n- `fetch` instead of jQuery AJAX\\n\\n- uniform behaviour in most browsers\\n\\nConclusion\\njQuery was useful in the past, but in modern applications the native DOM API or frameworks are more often used. Its application is justified in legacy projects but not in new development.","// jQuery\\n$(\'#button\')\\n\\n// The native API\\ndocument.querySelector(\'#button\')"],[1329,"The browser\'s event model describes how clicks, the typing of text and other actions of the user are handled. Events propagate through the DOM tree in a particular order. This lets you manage the interface\'s reaction to the user\'s actions. The model is needed for the flexible and predictable handling of events. It lies at the foundation of the interactivity of web applications.","The browser uses an event model so that the elements of a page can react to the user\'s actions and to changes in the system.\\nWhat an event is\\nAn event is a signal that an action has taken place — for example:\\n\\n- a click of the mouse\\n\\n- the typing of text\\n\\n- the loading of the page\\n\\n- a change of the window\'s size\\n\\nEvents are bound to DOM elements by means of handlers.\\nThe phases of the propagation of an event\\nAn event passes through several phases:\\n\\n- The capture phase — the event goes from `window` to the target element\\n\\n- The target phase — the event reaches the element\\n\\n- The bubbling phase — the event rises back up through the DOM\\n\\nBy default the handlers fire in the bubbling phase.\\nWhat the event model is for\\nIt solves several tasks:\\n\\n- it lets the user\'s actions be handled\\n\\n- it supports the delegation of events\\n\\n- it makes the interface\'s behaviour predictable\\n\\n- it simplifies the management of complex hierarchies of elements\\n\\nAn example of delegation:\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe browser\'s event model is the foundation of the interactive web. An understanding of the phases and of the propagation of events lets you write more efficient and more manageable code.","document.body.addEventListener(\'click\', (event) => {\\n  if (event.target.matches(\'button\')) {\\n    // the handling of the click on the button\\n  }\\n})"],[1330,"To obtain data by ID, `fetch` is used inside an effect that is carried out at the start of the application. The ID can be taken from the URL or from the configuration. The request is sent after the component has mounted. The response is converted into JSON and saved in the state. Such an approach lets you load the data once at the start.","The loading of data by ID is often required for detail pages or for an application\'s initial state.\\nObtaining the ID\\nThe ID is usually determined in one of these ways:\\n\\n- from the URL\\n\\n- from the route\'s parameters\\n\\n- from configuration known in advance\\n\\nAn example of a URL parameter:\\n\\nThe carrying out of the request\\nThe request is carried out after the component has mounted:\\n\\nThe handling of the states\\nIn the initialisation it matters to take into account:\\n\\n- the state of loading\\n\\n- errors of the network\\n\\n- the absence of data\\n\\nThis makes the interface robust to failures.\\nConclusion\\nThe obtaining of data by ID at the start of an application is implemented through `fetch` and effects. Such an approach is simple, clear and suitable for most scenarios of initial loading.","const id = \'42\' // notionally obtained from the URL\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  fetch(`/api/items/${id}`)\\n    .then(response => response.json())\\n    .then(data => setItem(data))\\n}, [id])"],[1331,"A good test checks the behaviour rather than the implementation. It has to be comprehensible and deterministic and give the same result on every run. A test should not depend on external factors or be fragile to changes in the code. It also matters that the tests are carried out fast and read easily. Bad tests complicate the development and slow the team down.","Good tests help you change code confidently and find errors fast. For that, several key criteria are used in their writing.\\nThe main criteria of good tests\\n\\n- The checking of the behaviour rather than of the implementation\\nA test should answer the question \\"what does the code do\\" rather than \\"how does it do it\\".\\n\\n- Determinism\\nThe result of a test should not depend on:\\n\\n- the time\\n\\n- random values\\n\\n- the state of the environment\\n\\n- Isolation\\nEvery test should be independent and not affect the other tests.\\n\\n- Comprehensibility and readability\\nFrom the test it should be clear:\\n\\n- what is being checked\\n\\n- under what conditions\\n\\n- what result is expected\\n\\n- Minimal fragility\\nSmall changes in the code should not break a large quantity of tests.\\n\\nAn example of a good test\\n\\nConclusion\\nA good test is a simple, reliable and readable way of fixing a system\'s behaviour rather than its internal implementation.","expect(calculateTotal(2, 3)).toBe(5)"],[1332,"Automated tests help you discover errors at an early stage and prevent regressions. They let you bring changes into code safely. The tests serve as additional documentation of the application\'s behaviour. They also raise developers\' confidence in refactoring. In the long term automated tests save the team\'s time.","Front-end applications grow fast and change often, so manual checking becomes unreliable. Automated tests solve that problem.\\nThe main reasons for the use of automated tests\\n\\n- The prevention of regressions\\nThe old functionality carries on working after changes.\\n\\n- Confidence in refactoring\\nYou can change code without fearing hidden breakages.\\n\\n- Fast feedback\\nErrors are discovered right after the changes.\\n\\n- The documenting of the behaviour\\nThe tests show how the system should work.\\n\\n- The raising of the product\'s quality\\nFewer bugs reach the user.\\n\\nAn example of a scenario\\n\\nConclusion\\nAutomated tests are a tool of stability and of speed of development rather than simply a check of code for errors.","// the test confirms the expected behaviour of the button\\nfireEvent.click(button)\\nexpect(onSubmit).toHaveBeenCalled()"],[1333,"A closure is formed at the moment of the creation of the function rather than of its call. The function remembers the lexical environment in which it was declared. Thanks to that it has access to the outer variables even after their execution has finished. This is a basic mechanism of the language. It is widely used in practice.","Closures are a fundamental mechanism of JavaScript, connected with lexical scope.\\nThe definition\\nA closure is a function together with the lexical environment in which it was created.\\nWhen a closure is formed\\n\\n- During the declaration of the function\\nAt that moment the function \\"remembers\\" the outer scope.\\n\\n- Independently of the place of the call\\nEven if the function is called in another place in the program, it carries on referring to the original environment.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nHere the inner function preserves access to `count`, although `createCounter` has already finished its execution.\\nThe practical application\\n\\n- the encapsulation of state\\n\\n- factories of functions\\n\\n- event handlers\\n\\n- memoization\\n\\nConclusion\\nA closure is formed at the creation of the function and preserves the connection with its lexical environment. This lets functions work with outer data even after the outer context has finished.","function createCounter() {\\n  let count = 0\\n\\n  return function () {\\n    count++\\n    return count\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst counter = createCounter()\\ncounter() // 1\\ncounter() // 2"],[1334,"A function obtains access to an outer scope thanks to lexical scope. At the creation of the function JavaScript remembers the environment in which it was declared. On a reference to a variable the interpreter searches for it first inside the function, then in the outer scopes. That search goes along the chain of scopes. This mechanism lies at the foundation of closures.","A function\'s access to outer variables is the result of the working of the lexical model of scopes in JavaScript.\\nHow the access to the outer data is formed\\nWhen a function is created, the JavaScript engine:\\n\\n- Fixes the lexical environment\\nThe function is \\"bound\\" to the scope in which it was declared rather than called.\\n\\n- Creates a reference to the outer environment\\nThat reference is used in the search for variables.\\n\\n- Uses the chain of scopes\\nIf a variable is not found locally, the search continues higher up.\\n\\nThe order of the search for a variable\\nThe search takes place in sequence:\\n\\n- the function\'s local variables\\n\\n- the function\'s parameters\\n\\n- the variables of the outer function or block\\n\\n- the global scope\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nThe function `inner` obtains access to `value` from `outer`, because it was precisely there that it was declared.\\nConclusion\\nA function obtains access to an outer scope through the lexical environment fixed at the moment of its creation. This mechanism makes the behaviour of functions predictable and independent of the place of the call.","const value = 10\\n\\nfunction outer() {\\n  const value = 20\\n\\n  function inner() {\\n    return value\\n  }\\n\\n  return inner\\n}\\n\\nconst fn = outer()\\nfn() // 20"],[1335,"The lexical environment is an internal structure in which variables and references to the outer scopes are held. It is created on entry into a block of code, a function or a module. It is precisely through the lexical environment that JavaScript resolves the names of variables. The user does not work with it directly, but it determines the behaviour of the code. Without it closures are impossible.","The lexical environment is a basic concept for an understanding of the working of JavaScript under the hood.\\nThe definition\\nThe lexical environment is a data structure that contains:\\n\\n- records of the variables and functions\\n\\n- a reference to the outer lexical environment\\n\\nWhat the lexical environment consists of\\n\\n- The Environment Record\\nIt holds:\\n\\n- the variables\\n\\n- the functions\' parameters\\n\\n- the declarations of functions\\n\\n- The reference to the outer environment\\nIt indicates where to search for variables if they are not found locally.\\n\\nWhen a lexical environment is created\\n\\n- on the execution of the script\\n\\n- on the call of a function\\n\\n- on entry into a `{}` block with `let` and `const`\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nFor `inner` a lexical environment is created containing a reference to the environment of `example`.\\nThe connection with closures\\nA closure arises when a function preserves access to a lexical environment after it has been left.\\nConclusion\\nThe lexical environment is the foundation of the mechanism of scopes in JavaScript. It determines which variables are available at a specific point in the program\'s execution.","function example() {\\n  let x = 5\\n\\n  function inner() {\\n    return x\\n  }\\n\\n  return inner()\\n}"],[1336,"A compiled language is converted into machine code before the program is carried out. An interpreted language is carried out line by line at the time of the run. This affects the performance and the way errors are discovered. In practice the boundary between these approaches is blurred. JavaScript uses a hybrid model.","The difference between interpreted and compiled languages is connected with when and how the code is converted for execution.\\nCompiled languages\\nIn such languages:\\n\\n- the source code is completely compiled in advance\\n\\n- an executable file is created\\n\\n- errors are more often detected before the run\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- high performance\\n\\n- the early discovery of errors\\n\\nThe disadvantages:\\n\\n- a separate step of the build\\n\\n- less flexibility at the run\\n\\nInterpreted languages\\nIn such languages:\\n\\n- the code is analysed and carried out at the time of the run\\n\\n- there is no separate executable file\\n\\n- errors may arise during the execution\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- a fast start of the development\\n\\n- flexibility and dynamism\\n\\nThe disadvantages:\\n\\n- potentially lower performance\\n\\nWhere JavaScript is\\nJavaScript:\\n\\n- is not compiled in advance in the classical sense\\n\\n- is interpreted and optimised by the engine\\n\\n- uses JIT compilation\\n\\nThis lets it combine the flexibility of interpretation and a high speed of execution.\\nConclusion\\nInterpreted and compiled languages differ in the moment of the conversion of the code. JavaScript uses a hybrid approach, which makes it dynamic and sufficiently performant.",null],[1337,"Imports and exports let you define explicitly which parts of a module are available from outside. The entities exported are declared in one file and imported in another. The connection between the modules is created at the stage of the loading rather than during the execution of the functions. Modules are carried out once and cached. This makes code predictable and structured.","JavaScript\'s module system is founded on explicit dependencies between files and strict rules of their linking.\\nThe main principles of the working of modules\\n\\n- The explicit export\\nThe module itself defines which functions or values are available from outside.\\n\\n- The import by reference rather than by copying\\nThe entity imported is a reference to the export rather than a copy of it.\\n\\n- The one-off execution of the module\\nEvery module is carried out only once, even if it is imported in several places.\\n\\n- Static analysis\\nThe imports and exports are analysed before the code is carried out, which lets you:\\n\\n- do tree shaking\\n\\n- detect circular dependencies\\n\\nThe order of the execution\\n\\n- first the dependencies are loaded\\n\\n- then the code of the modules is carried out\\n\\n- after that the exported values become available\\n\\nConclusion\\nImports and exports form explicit contracts between files and let you manage dependencies at the level of the application\'s structure rather than during the execution.","export function sum(a: number, b: number) {\\n  return a + b\\n}\\n\\nimport { sum } from \'./math\'"],[1338,"Yes, an imported function completely preserves access to its own lexical scope. The import does not affect the environment in which the function was declared. The function always works with the variables that were available to it at the moment of its creation. The place of the call has no significance. This follows from JavaScript\'s lexical model.","The import of a function from another file does not change its behaviour from the point of view of scopes.\\nWhy the access is preserved\\n\\n- The function is created in its own module\\nThe lexical environment is fixed at the moment of the function\'s declaration.\\n\\n- The import passes a reference to the function\\nTogether with the function the reference to its environment is preserved.\\n\\n- The place of the call does not affect the scope\\nThe function is not \\"rebound\\" to the file in which it was imported.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nThe variable `count` remains part of the lexical environment of the `counter` module.\\nThe practical consequences\\n\\n- modules can encapsulate state\\n\\n- hidden side effects are possible\\n\\n- it matters to work carefully with shared state\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn imported function always has access to its original lexical scope. This makes modules predictable, but it requires an attentive attitude to state.","// counter.ts\\nlet count = 0\\n\\nexport function increment() {\\n  count++\\n  return count\\n}\\n\\n// app.ts\\nimport { increment } from \'./counter\'\\n\\nincrement() // 1\\nincrement() // 2"],[1339,"An incorrect cache can lead to outdated data and to bugs that are hard to catch. Problems with the invalidation of the cache and with incorrect keys often arise. Memory leaks and a mismatch of the data with the real state are possible. Such errors are difficult to debug. A cache therefore requires a careful implementation.","Caching speeds an application\'s working up, but with errors in the implementation it can create more problems than benefit.\\nThe typical errors in the implementation of a cache\\n\\n- Outdated data\\nThe cache is not updated when the original source of the data changes.\\n\\n- Incorrect keys\\nDifferent data may be saved under one key, which leads to wrong results.\\n\\n- The absence of a strategy of invalidation\\nIt is unclear:\\n\\n- when to clear the cache\\n\\n- what data to consider out of date\\n\\n- Memory leaks\\nThe cache grows without limits and is never cleared.\\n\\n- Hidden logical errors\\nThe code begins to depend on the presence of the cache rather than on the real data.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nSuch code can break if the cached value is equal to `0` or `false`.\\nConclusion\\nCaching requires a well-thought-out strategy of keys, of updating and of clearing. Without that a cache becomes a source of unstable errors that are hard to track down.","if (cache[key]) {\\n  return cache[key]\\n}"],[1340,"A check through falsiness breaks when the cached value is permissible but logically false. The values `0`, `false`, `\'\'` and `null` are interpreted as the absence of data. As a result the cache is ignored and the data is recomputed anew. This leads to superfluous computations and to logical errors. The problem often remains unnoticed.","In JavaScript many values are coerced into `false` in a logical context, and that creates hidden problems in work with a cache.\\nWhat counts as falsy\\nThe falsy values include:\\n\\n- `false`\\n\\n- `0`\\n\\n- `\'\'`\\n\\n- `null`\\n\\n- `undefined`\\n\\n- `NaN`\\n\\nWhere the problem arises\\nIf the cache holds such values, a check of the form:\\n\\nworks incorrectly, because:\\n\\n- the value is in the cache\\n\\n- but the condition is not met\\n\\nThe typical consequences\\n\\n- Repeated computations\\nThe data is recomputed although it is already in the cache.\\n\\n- The violation of the business logic\\n`false` or `0` may be a valid result but are perceived as the absence of a value.\\n\\n- Bugs that are hard to find\\nThe error shows itself only with particular data.\\n\\nThe correct approach\\nYou have to check the very fact of the presence of the key:\\n\\nor compare with `undefined`.\\nConclusion\\nA check through falsiness is unsafe for a cache. It is more correct to check the presence of the key rather than the logical value of the data.","if (cache[key]) {\\n  return cache[key]\\n}\\n\\nif (key in cache) {\\n  return cache[key]\\n}"],[1341,"The `Promise` constructor is carried out synchronously right at the creation. The calls of `then`, `catch` and `finally` register the callbacks synchronously. But the callbacks themselves are carried out asynchronously. They land in the queue of microtasks. This guarantees a predictable order of the execution.","A Promise combines synchronous and asynchronous behaviour, which often causes confusion.\\nThe synchronous parts of a Promise\\n\\n- The call of the `Promise` constructor\\nThe executor function is run at once.\\n\\n- The registration of the handlers\\nThe calls of `then`, `catch` and `finally` are carried out at once and simply add the callbacks.\\n\\nThe asynchronous parts of a Promise\\n\\n- The execution of the `then` and `catch` handlers\\nEven if the Promise is already resolved, the callback will be carried out asynchronously.\\n\\n- The queue of microtasks\\nA Promise\'s callbacks always land in the microtask queue.\\n\\nAn example of the order of the execution\\n\\nThe result:\\n\\n- `start`\\n\\n- `end`\\n\\n- `then`\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- it affects the order of the execution of the logic\\n\\n- it helps you avoid race conditions\\n\\n- it matters in the debugging of asynchronous code\\n\\nConclusion\\nA Promise is created and configured synchronously, but its handlers are always carried out asynchronously through the queue of microtasks.","new Promise((resolve) => {\\n  console.log(\'sync\')\\n  resolve(1)\\n})\\n\\nconsole.log(\'start\')\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\\n  console.log(\'then\')\\n})\\n\\nconsole.log(\'end\')"],[1342,"`Promise.then` lands in the microtask queue in order to be carried out right after the current synchronous code. This gives it a higher priority compared with macrotasks. Such a mechanism ensures the predictability and the consistency of asynchronous behaviour. It lets a Promise work faster than the timers. This is an important part of the JavaScript specification.","JavaScript uses several queues of tasks, and a Promise has a particular priority.\\nWhat microtasks are\\nMicrotasks are a queue of tasks that:\\n\\n- is carried out after the current call stack has finished\\n\\n- is emptied completely before the move to the next macrotask\\n\\nWhy Promises use microtasks\\n\\n- A predictable order of the execution\\nPromise callbacks are carried out before:\\n\\n- `setTimeout`\\n\\n- `setInterval`\\n\\n- DOM events\\n\\n- The integrity of the asynchronous chains\\nChains of `then` are carried out without the interference of other tasks.\\n\\n- Consistency with the specification\\nSuch behaviour is guaranteed by the standard and is identical in every engine.\\n\\nAn example of a comparison\\n\\nThe result:\\n\\n- `promise`\\n\\n- `timeout`\\n\\nThe practical significance\\n\\n- it matters in complex asynchronous scenarios\\n\\n- it helps you avoid an unexpected order of execution\\n\\n- it is critical for libraries and frameworks\\n\\nConclusion\\n`Promise.then` is carried out in the microtask queue in order to guarantee a high priority and a predictable order of the execution of asynchronous code.","setTimeout(() => console.log(\'timeout\'), 0)\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\'promise\'))"],[1343,"First all of the synchronous code is always carried out. Then the microtasks are carried out, the handlers of `Promise.then` included. Only after that are the macrotasks handled, such as `setTimeout`. The output to the console therefore often differs from intuitive expectations. This behaviour is strictly defined by the specification.","In the mixing of synchronous code and a Promise it matters to understand the priorities of the queues of execution.\\nThe general order of the execution\\nJavaScript carries code out in the following order:\\n\\n- The synchronous code of the current stack\\n\\n- The queue of microtasks\\n\\n- The queue of macrotasks\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nThe step-by-step analysis\\n\\n- `console.log(\'A\')` is carried out\\n\\n- The `then` is registered but not carried out\\n\\n- `console.log(\'C\')` is carried out\\n\\n- The queue of microtasks is emptied\\n\\n- `console.log(\'B\')` is carried out\\n\\nThe result in the console:\\n\\n- `A`\\n\\n- `C`\\n\\n- `B`\\n\\nThe frequent mistakes\\n\\n- the expectation that the `then` will be carried out at once\\n\\n- confusion between a `Promise` and a `setTimeout`\\n\\n- wrong assumptions about the order of the logs\\n\\nConclusion\\nIn the mixing of a Promise and synchronous code the synchronous part is always carried out first, then the microtasks. An understanding of this order is critical for correct asynchronous logic.","console.log(\'A\')\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\\n  console.log(\'B\')\\n})\\n\\nconsole.log(\'C\')"],[1344,"Arrow functions do not create a `this` of their own because they take it from the outer lexical scope. Such behaviour makes them predictable and spares you the need to use `bind`. This was done deliberately in order to simplify the work with callbacks. The context is determined at the moment of the function\'s creation. It is impossible to change it.","Arrow functions were added to JavaScript in order to simplify the work with functions and with the context.\\nHow this works in arrow functions\\n\\n- The absence of a context of their own\\nAn arrow function does not create:\\n\\n- `this`\\n\\n- `arguments`\\n\\n- `super`\\n\\n- The lexical binding of this\\nThe value of `this` is taken from the outer lexical environment.\\n\\n- The fixing at the creation\\nThe context is determined at the moment of the function\'s declaration rather than of its call.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nHere the arrow function uses the `this` from `Timer` rather than from `setInterval`.\\nThe practical consequences\\n\\n- `call`, `apply` and `bind` cannot be used\\n\\n- it is convenient for handlers and callbacks\\n\\n- it is not suitable for the methods of objects\\n\\nConclusion\\nArrow functions use a lexical `this`, which makes their behaviour stable and convenient in asynchronous code.","function Timer() {\\n  this.seconds = 0\\n\\n  setInterval(() => {\\n    this.seconds++\\n  }, 1000)\\n}"],[1345,"Classes provide a more declarative and more readable syntax for work with prototypes. Constructor functions require manual work with the `prototype`. Classes introduce limitations — the prohibition of a call without `new`, for example. The same prototypal model is used under the hood, however. The difference is mainly syntactic and behavioural.","In JavaScript classes and constructor functions solve one task, but they do it in different ways.\\nThe main differences\\n\\n- The syntax\\nA class looks better structured and more familiar to developers from other languages.\\n\\n- The limitations of the call\\nA class cannot be called without `new`.\\nA constructor function can be, which often leads to errors.\\n\\n- The work with methods\\nA class\'s methods are automatically added to the `prototype`.\\n\\n- Hoisting\\nClasses are not hoisted in the same way as functions.\\n\\nWhat they have in common\\n\\n- prototypal inheritance is used\\n\\n- the instances work identically\\n\\n- there is no genuine classical OOP\\n\\nConclusion\\nClasses are a stricter and more convenient syntax on top of constructor functions. They improve the readability of code, but they do not change JavaScript\'s basic model.","class User {\\n  constructor(name: string) {\\n    this.name = name\\n  }\\n}"],[1346,"Yes, classes in JavaScript are syntactic sugar over the prototypal model. Under the hood they use the same constructor functions and `prototype`. Classes do not introduce a new object model but merely simplify the syntax. They do add some limitations and particulars of behaviour, however. This makes code safer and more readable.","Classes in JavaScript are often perceived as a full implementation of classical OOP, but at the level of the language that is not so.\\nWhy classes are considered syntactic sugar\\nClasses:\\n\\n- Use prototypes\\nAll of a class\'s methods are in fact written into `ClassName.prototype`.\\n\\n- Are founded on constructor functions\\nA class\'s constructor is an ordinary function, called through `new`.\\n\\n- Do not introduce a new model of inheritance\\nInheritance still works through the chain of prototypes.\\n\\nWhat classes nevertheless add\\n\\n- Stricter rules\\n\\n- a class cannot be called without `new`\\n\\n- a class\'s code is always carried out in strict mode\\n\\n- Improved readability\\nThe structure of a class explicitly shows:\\n\\n- the constructor\\n\\n- the methods\\n\\n- the inheritance\\n\\n- A single syntax for inheritance\\n\\nWhat classes do not change\\n\\n- the way the methods are held\\n\\n- the mechanism of `this`\\n\\n- the prototypal nature of objects\\n\\nConclusion\\nClasses in JavaScript are syntactic sugar over prototypes with additional limitations. They improve the readability and the safety of code, but they do not change the language\'s fundamental principles.","class Admin extends User {\\n  getRole() {\\n    return \'admin\'\\n  }\\n}"],[1347,"The `this` context is determined at the moment of the function\'s call rather than of its declaration. On the assignment of a method to a variable the connection with the object is lost. As a result the function is called as an ordinary one, without a context. This leads to `undefined` or to the global object. Such behaviour is part of the JavaScript specification.","The loss of `this` is one of the most frequent problems in JavaScript, especially in work with the methods of objects.\\nHow this is determined\\nThe value of `this` depends on the form of the call:\\n\\n- An object\'s method\\n`this` points to the object to the left of the dot.\\n\\n- An ordinary call of a function\\n`this` is equal to `undefined` in strict mode.\\n\\nWhere the problem arises\\nWhen a method is assigned to a variable:\\n\\nThe following takes place:\\n\\n- the method is extracted from the object\\n\\n- the connection with `user` is lost\\n\\n- the call becomes an ordinary one\\n\\nWhy this is logical\\nJavaScript does not bind `this` to a function for ever.\\nThe context is determined exclusively by the way of the call.\\nThe ways of avoiding the problem\\n\\n- use `bind`\\n\\n- call the method through the object\\n\\n- use arrow functions as the callbacks\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe `this` context is lost on the assignment of a method to a variable because the function is called without an owner object. This is a fundamental rule of the language.","const user = {\\n  name: \'Alex\',\\n  getName() {\\n    return this.name\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst fn = user.getName\\nfn()"],[1348,"The `bind` method creates a new function with `this` rigidly fixed. The value of the context no longer depends on the way of the call. A repeated `bind` does not change `this`. This is convenient for passing methods as callbacks. The original function is thereby not changed.","`bind` is used when you have to guarantee a specific context of a function\'s execution.\\nHow bind works\\nOn the call of `bind`:\\n\\n- a new function is created\\n\\n- `this` is bound for ever to the object passed\\n\\n- the arguments can be partially fixed\\n\\nThe particulars of bind\\n\\n- The impossibility of overriding this\\n`call` and `apply` no longer affect the context.\\n\\n- A new function is returned\\nThe original function remains unchanged.\\n\\n- It is suitable for callbacks\\nIt is often used in event handlers and timers.\\n\\nThe limitations\\n\\n- it cannot be used for arrow functions\\n\\n- it complicates the debugging with excessive use\\n\\nConclusion\\n`bind` fixes `this` for ever for a specific function. This is a reliable way of avoiding the loss of the context in the passing of functions.","const user = {\\n  name: \'Alex\'\\n}\\n\\nfunction showName() {\\n  return this.name\\n}\\n\\nconst bound = showName.bind(user)\\nbound() // \'Alex\'"],[1349,"The `some` method iterates over an array\'s elements and returns `true` if at least one element satisfies the condition. The implementation comes down to a loop with an early exit. The callback is called for every element up to the first match. If the condition is not met even once, `false` is returned. The behaviour is strictly defined by the specification.","The implementation of `some` helps you understand how the built-in array methods work and why they are arranged precisely as they are.\\nThe general principle of the working\\nThe `some` method:\\n\\n- passes over the array from left to right\\n\\n- calls the callback for every element\\n\\n- stops at the first `true`\\n\\n- returns `true` or `false`\\n\\nThe simplest implementation\\n\\nThe key points\\n\\n- a loop is used rather than `forEach`\\n\\n- there is an early exit\\n\\n- the order of the elements is preserved\\n\\nConclusion\\n`some` is a linear pass over an array with the possibility of an early finish. Its manual implementation demonstrates the working of callbacks and conditions in JavaScript well.","function some<T>(array: T[], callback: (value: T) => boolean): boolean {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {\\n    if (callback(array[i])) {\\n      return true\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return false\\n}"],[1350,"The callback of `some` takes three arguments: the current element, its index and the original array. You can use not all of the arguments but only the ones you need. The order of the arguments is fixed. This behaviour is identical for most of the array methods. A knowledge of the signature simplifies the writing of custom implementations.","The callback of the `some` function is called with a set of arguments defined in advance.\\nThe signature of the callback\\nThe callback takes:\\n\\n- The current element\\nThe value of the array\'s element.\\n\\n- The element\'s index\\nThe element\'s position in the array.\\n\\n- The original array\\nA reference to the array over which the iteration is going.\\n\\nAn example of the use\\n\\nThe practical applications of the arguments\\n\\n- `value` — the main check of the condition\\n\\n- `index` — logic depending on the position\\n\\n- `array` — a comparison with the other elements\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe callback of the `some` method always receives the element, the index and the array. This makes the method flexible and fit for complex conditions.","[1, 2, 3].some((value, index, array) => {\\n  return value > array.length\\n})"],[1351,"The most reliable way is to use `typeof`. For functions it returns the string `\'function\'`. This is standard behaviour of the language. The check is simple and fast. It is enough in most cases.","The checking of arguments is an important part of defensive programming, especially in utility functions.\\nThe basic way of checking\\n\\nWhy typeof precisely\\n\\n- Standard behaviour\\n`typeof` correctly determines functions.\\n\\n- Simplicity and readability\\nThe check is easily comprehended when reading the code.\\n\\n- Predictability\\nIt works identically in every environment.\\n\\nThe alternatives and their limitations\\n\\n- `instanceof Function` depends on the context of the execution\\n\\n- a check through `Object.prototype.toString` is excessive\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo check that an argument is a function it is enough to use `typeof`. This is the simplest and most reliable way.","if (typeof callback !== \'function\') {\\n  throw new TypeError(\'Argument must be a function\')\\n}"],[1352,"An error should be thrown right at the discovery of an incorrect argument. `throw` and the standard classes of error, such as `TypeError` or `Error`, are usually used. The error\'s message has to be comprehensible and describe the problem. This simplifies the debugging and prevents the execution of incorrect logic. Such an approach is considered good practice.","The correct handling of errors begins with the explicit checking of the input data.\\nThe general principles of the throwing of errors\\n\\n- The early checking of the arguments\\nThe check has to be carried out at the beginning of the function, before the main logic.\\n\\n- The use of a suitable type of error\\n\\n- `TypeError` — if the argument is of the wrong type\\n\\n- `RangeError` — if the value is outside the permissible range\\n\\n- `Error` — for the general cases\\n\\n- A comprehensible error message\\nThe message has to explain:\\n\\n- which argument is incorrect\\n\\n- what value was expected\\n\\nAn example of a correct implementation\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- the errors are discovered at once\\n\\n- the code becomes predictable\\n\\n- the debugging and the maintenance are simplified\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe correct throwing of errors is the explicit checking of the arguments and the use of suitable types of error with comprehensible messages.","function some<T>(array: T[], callback: (value: T) => boolean): boolean {\\n  if (!Array.isArray(array)) {\\n    throw new TypeError(\'First argument must be an array\')\\n  }\\n\\n  if (typeof callback !== \'function\') {\\n    throw new TypeError(\'Second argument must be a function\')\\n  }\\n\\n  for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {\\n    if (callback(array[i])) {\\n      return true\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  return false\\n}"],[1353,"The index is available directly from the counter of the loop used in the traversal of the array. It has to be passed as the second argument into the callback. This completely repeats the behaviour of the standard `some` method. Such an approach is simple and clear. It requires no additional data structures.","In the manual implementation of `some` the element\'s index is always known, since the iteration over the array is carried out through a loop.\\nHow to pass the index into the callback\\n\\n- Use the loop\'s index\\nThe loop\'s variable contains the current index of the element.\\n\\n- Pass the index as the second argument\\nThe callback is called with the same set of arguments as in the standard implementation.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nThe practical benefit of the index\\n\\n- logic depending on the element\'s position\\n\\n- access to the neighbouring elements\\n\\n- debugging and logging\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe element\'s index in a custom implementation of `some` is taken from the loop and passed into the callback as the second argument, completely repeating the standard behaviour.","function some<T>(\\n  array: T[],\\n  callback: (value: T, index: number) => boolean\\n): boolean {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {\\n    if (callback(array[i], i)) {\\n      return true\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return false\\n}"],[1354,"The array itself is passed into the callback as the third argument. This is standard behaviour of JavaScript\'s array methods. Thanks to that the callback has access to the whole array. Such an approach lets you write more flexible conditions. It is used in every method of iteration.","The built-in array methods pass into the callback not only the element but also the context of the iteration.\\nThe standard signature of the callback\\nThe callback receives the arguments in the following order:\\n\\n- the current element\\n\\n- the element\'s index\\n\\n- the original array\\n\\nThe implementation in a custom some\\n\\nWhat the access to the array is for\\n\\n- the comparison of the current element with the others\\n\\n- the checking of the array\'s length\\n\\n- complex conditions on the basis of the context\\n\\nConclusion\\nAccess to the array inside a callback is implemented by the passing of the array as the third argument. This is the standard and expected contract of the array methods.","function some<T>(\\n  array: T[],\\n  callback: (value: T, index: number, array: T[]) => boolean\\n): boolean {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {\\n    if (callback(array[i], i, array)) {\\n      return true\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return false\\n}"],[1355,"`some` checks whether there exists at least one element satisfying the condition. `every` checks that the condition is met for every element. `filter` returns a new array with the elements that passed the check. These methods solve different tasks, although they use a similar callback. The wrong choice of method leads to superfluous computations and less readable code.","The methods `some`, `every` and `filter` look similar, but they are intended for different scenarios.\\nThe purpose of each method\\n\\n- some\\nIt returns `true` if at least one element satisfies the condition.\\nIt is used for the checking of the fact of the existence of a suitable element.\\n\\n- every\\nIt returns `true` only if every element of the array passes the check.\\nIt is suitable for the validation of data or the checking of invariants.\\n\\n- filter\\nIt returns a new array of the elements satisfying the condition.\\nIt is used when you need the result rather than simply a boolean value.\\n\\nThe key differences in the behaviour\\n\\n- The return value\\n\\n- `some` -> `boolean`\\n\\n- `every` -> `boolean`\\n\\n- `filter` -> `array`\\n\\n- The early finish\\n\\n- `some` stops at the first `true`\\n\\n- `every` stops at the first `false`\\n\\n- `filter` always passes over the whole array\\n\\n- Performance\\nFor simple checks `some` and `every` are preferable, since they can finish earlier.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nConclusion\\n`some` and `every` are used for logical checks, and `filter` for obtaining data. The choice of the method has to correspond to the goal, otherwise the code becomes less efficient and less readable.","const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4]\\n\\nnumbers.some(n => n > 3)   // true\\nnumbers.every(n => n > 0)  // true\\nnumbers.filter(n => n > 2) // [3, 4]"],[1356,"The `some` method finishes early when the callback returns `true`. After that the further iteration over the array ceases. This lets you avoid superfluous computations. Such behaviour is written into the specification. It makes the method efficient for checks.","One of the key particulars of `some` is the possibility of the early finish of the iteration over the array.\\nThe condition of the early finish\\n`some` ceases its execution if:\\n\\n- the callback has returned `true` for at least one element\\n\\n- the further checking becomes pointless\\n\\nHow this looks in practice\\n\\nThe order of the execution:\\n\\n- `1` is checked -> `false`\\n\\n- `2` is checked -> `false`\\n\\n- `3` is checked -> `true`\\n\\n- the iteration stops\\n\\n`4` will no longer be handled.\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- it raises the performance\\n\\n- it reduces the number of calls of the callback\\n\\n- it lets `some` be used as a logical check\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe `some` method always finishes early at the first `true` from the callback. This makes it an efficient tool for searches and for the checking of conditions in an array.","[1, 2, 3, 4].some(value => {\\n  console.log(value)\\n  return value > 2\\n})"],[1357,"`cookie` and `sessionStorage` are used for holding data in the browser.\\nCookies are automatically sent to the server with every HTTP request.\\n`sessionStorage` is available only on the client and lives within the bounds of one tab.\\nThey differ in their volume, their lifetime and their area of application.","In a browser there are several mechanisms for holding data, each of which solves its own tasks.\\nThe definition\\nA cookie is a small piece of data that the browser holds and sends to the server with every request.\\nsessionStorage is a client-side store of data, existing within the bounds of one tab of the browser.\\nThe main differences\\nCookies and sessionStorage differ in key parameters:\\n\\n- The scope\\n\\n- Cookies are available to the server and to the client\\n\\n- sessionStorage is available only in the browser\\n\\n- The lifetime\\n\\n- Cookies can have a set term of life\\n\\n- sessionStorage is cleared when the tab is closed\\n\\n- The sending to the server\\n\\n- Cookies are sent automatically\\n\\n- sessionStorage takes no part in HTTP requests\\n\\n- The volume of data\\n\\n- Cookies are limited in size\\n\\n- sessionStorage holds more data\\n\\nAn example of the use\\n\\nConclusion\\nCookies are used for server-side scenarios — authorisation, for example. sessionStorage is suitable for temporary client-side state inside a tab.","sessionStorage.setItem(\'theme\', \'dark\');"],[1358,"`sessionStorage` is used for holding data within the bounds of one tab of the browser.\\nThe data is preserved on a refresh of the page but is deleted when the tab is closed.\\nIt is suitable for temporary UI state.\\nIt is often applied where the data should not be preserved for long.","sessionStorage solves the task of the short-lived holding of data on the client without the participation of the server.\\nThe purpose of sessionStorage\\nThis mechanism is suitable for data that:\\n\\n- Is needed only within the bounds of one tab\\n\\n- Should not be transferred to the server\\n\\n- Should not be preserved after the tab is closed\\n\\nThe typical scenarios of use\\nIn practice sessionStorage is applied for:\\n\\n- Temporary filters and settings of the interface\\n\\n- The data of multi-step forms\\n\\n- UI flags (a modal is open, a tab is chosen)\\n\\n- A cache of intermediate computations\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nThe limitations\\n\\n- The data is not shared between tabs\\n\\n- It is completely cleared when the tab is closed\\n\\n- It is not suitable for long-term storage\\n\\nConclusion\\nsessionStorage is used for short-lived client-side state. This is a simple and safe way of holding data within the bounds of one tab\'s session.","sessionStorage.setItem(\'step\', \'2\');\\nconst step = sessionStorage.getItem(\'step\');"],[1359,"`==` compares values with the coercion of types.\\n`===` compares both the value and the type without coercion.\\n`===` is more predictable and safer.\\nIn modern code it is recommended that you use `===`.","In JavaScript there are two operators of comparison for equality, which behave differently.\\nThe definition\\n`==` (loose equality) compares values with the automatic coercion of types.\\n`===` (strict equality) compares the value and the type without coercion.\\nHow `==` works\\nIn a `==` comparison JavaScript may convert the types:\\n\\n- a string into a number\\n\\n- `null` and `undefined`\\n\\n- boolean values\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nHow `===` works\\n`===` carries out no conversions:\\n\\nWhy `===` is preferable\\n\\n- Predictable behaviour\\n\\n- Fewer hidden errors\\n\\n- The code is simpler to read and maintain\\n\\nConclusion\\n`===` should be used by default. `==` is permissible only with a complete understanding of the rules of the coercion of types.","0 == false      // true\\n\'\' == 0         // true\\nnull == undefined // true\\n\\n0 === false     // false\\n\'\' === 0        // false"],[1360,"In JavaScript every object has a reference to its prototype, available through `Object.getPrototypeOf`. The recursive traversal of the chain of prototypes consists in the successive obtaining of the prototype, then of the prototype of the prototype and so on, until `null` is reached. This is used for the search for properties or methods or for the analysis of the structure of the inheritance. Such an approach helps you understand where an object gets this or that behaviour from. It is also useful in debugging and in the writing of utilities.","The chain of prototypes determines the mechanism of inheritance in JavaScript. When a property is not found in the object itself, the search continues in its prototype and then further up the chain.\\nWhat the chain of prototypes is\\nThe chain of prototypes is a sequence of objects connected through the internal `[[Prototype]]` reference.\\n\\n- Every object has a prototype (except `Object.prototype`)\\n\\n- The top point of the chain is `null`\\n\\n- The search for properties takes place from the bottom up\\n\\nThe recursive traversal of the prototypes\\nFor the traversal the standard API is used.\\n\\nIn this example:\\n\\n- First the object itself is handled\\n\\n- Then its prototype\\n\\n- The traversal finishes when `null` is reached\\n\\nWhat this can be needed for\\nThe recursive traversal of the prototypes is applied in several cases.\\n\\n- Debugging\\n\\n- Understanding where a method came from\\n\\n- The analysis of unexpected overrides\\n\\n- Metaprogramming\\n\\n- The writing of polyfills\\n\\n- The creation of tools for the inspection of objects\\n\\n- The checking of the presence of a property\\n\\n- The search for a property without the use of `in`\\n\\n- Control of only the own or the inherited properties\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe recursive traversal of the chain of prototypes is useful for a deep understanding of the working of objects and of inheritance in JavaScript, especially in debugging and in the creation of auxiliary tools.","function walkPrototypeChain(obj) {\\n  if (obj === null) return;\\n  console.log(obj);\\n  walkPrototypeChain(Object.getPrototypeOf(obj));\\n}"],[1361,"A closure arises when an inner function remembers the variables from an outer function even after that function has finished. JavaScript preserves a reference to the lexical environment in which the function was created. Thanks to that the inner function can read and change those variables. Closures are often used for the encapsulation of data. This is one of the language\'s basic mechanisms.","Closures are a consequence of lexical scope in JavaScript. Functions remember the context in which they were declared.\\nThe definition\\nA closure is the combination of a function and the lexical environment in which it was created.\\nHow this works\\nWhen a function is created:\\n\\n- It receives a reference to the outer variables\\n\\n- Those variables are preserved in memory\\n\\n- They are not deleted after the outer function has finished\\n\\nAn example of a simple closure:\\n\\nHere:\\n\\n- `count` belongs to the outer function\\n\\n- The inner function preserves access to it\\n\\n- The value is not reset between the calls\\n\\nWhere closures are used\\n\\n- The encapsulation of state\\n\\n- The hiding of internal variables\\n\\n- Control of the access to the data\\n\\n- Factory functions\\n\\n- The creation of configurable functions\\n\\n- The reuse of logic\\n\\n- Event handlers and callbacks\\n\\n- The preservation of the context\\n\\n- Work with asynchronous code\\n\\nThe possible problems\\n\\n- Memory leaks on incorrect use\\n\\n- The difficulty of debugging with nested closures\\n\\nConclusion\\nClosures let functions preserve state and access to the variables of an outer context, which makes them a powerful tool for the management of data and of logic.","function createCounter() {\\n  let count = 0;\\n\\n  return function () {\\n    count++;\\n    return count;\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst counter = createCounter();\\ncounter(); // 1\\ncounter(); // 2"],[1362,"`v-if` is better used when the condition changes rarely or the component is heavy to paint. `v-show` is preferable if the element has to be shown and hidden often. `v-if` completely adds the element to and removes it from the DOM, while `v-show` merely changes its visibility through CSS. This affects the speed of the switchings and the initial loading. The choice depends on the scenario of use.","Although `v-if` and `v-show` solve one task, their application is optimal in different situations.\\nWhen to use v-if\\n`v-if` governs the creation and the destruction of DOM elements.\\nIts use is justified if:\\n\\n- The condition changes rarely\\n\\n- The component is complex or heavy\\n\\n- The resources have to be freed completely\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn that case:\\n\\n- The component is not created without need\\n\\n- No superfluous computations are carried out\\n\\n- No memory is spent on unused DOM\\n\\nWhen to use v-show\\n`v-show` governs only the element\'s visibility.\\nIts use is justified if:\\n\\n- The visibility is switched often\\n\\n- A fast display matters\\n\\n- The component has to preserve its state\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nHere:\\n\\n- The element is created once\\n\\n- The switching takes place fast\\n\\n- The component\'s state is preserved\\n\\nThe practical rule\\nBefore the choice of the directive it is worth asking the question:\\n\\n- Which is more expensive — the frequent switching or the initial paint?\\n\\nConclusion\\n`v-if` is suitable for rare conditions and heavy components, `v-show` for the frequent hiding and showing of elements without the recreation of the DOM.","<HeavyComponent v-if=\\"isAuthorized\\" />\\n\\n<div v-show=\\"isOpen\\">\\n  The menu\\n</div>"],[1363,"With the use of `keep-alive` components are not destroyed when they are hidden. Instead of the standard hooks of destruction, special hooks of activation and deactivation are used. These are `activated` and `deactivated`. They let you react to the repeated display of a component. Such a mechanism is used for the caching of state.","`keep-alive` is a built-in component of Vue, which caches dynamic components and preserves their state.\\nHow keep-alive works\\nWhen a component is wrapped in `keep-alive`:\\n\\n- The component is not destroyed on a change of the route\\n\\n- The DOM may be temporarily removed\\n\\n- The component\'s state is preserved in memory\\n\\nThe special hooks of keep-alive\\nFor work with cacheable components Vue provides additional hooks.\\n\\n- `activated`\\n\\n- It is called on the activation of the component\\n\\n- The analogue of a repeated display\\n\\n- It is suitable for the updating of the data\\n\\n- `deactivated`\\n\\n- It is called on the deactivation of the component\\n\\n- The component is hidden but not destroyed\\n\\n- It is used for the suspension of logic\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe difference from the ordinary lifecycle\\n\\n- `beforeUnmount` and `unmounted` are not called\\n\\n- The component remains in memory\\n\\n- The state is not reset\\n\\nConclusion\\nWith the use of `keep-alive` the lifecycle is supplemented with the `activated` and `deactivated` hooks, which let you manage the logic of cacheable components without destroying them.","export default {\\n  activated() {\\n    // the updating of the data on the return\\n  },\\n  deactivated() {\\n    // the pausing of the timers or the subscriptions\\n  }\\n};"],[1364,"Before the creation of the DOM tree in Vue the `beforeCreate` and `created` hooks are available. In `beforeCreate` you still cannot work with the data and the methods. In `created` the data, the computed properties and the methods are already available. The DOM does not yet exist at that stage, however. These hooks are used for the initialisation of logic not connected with the DOM.","Before the mounting of a component Vue passes through an initial stage of initialisation, at which the DOM has not yet been created.\\nThe beforeCreate hook\\nThis hook is called first of all.\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- The reactive data is not available\\n\\n- The methods and the computed properties have not yet been initialised\\n\\n- It is used extremely rarely\\n\\nAn example of the use is limited to logic not depending on the component\'s state.\\nThe created hook\\nAt this stage the initialisation of the data has finished.\\nThe capabilities:\\n\\n- `data`, `methods` and `computed` are available\\n\\n- You can subscribe to events\\n\\n- You can initiate the loading of data\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhat is not available at this stage\\n\\n- The DOM elements\\n\\n- The refs\\n\\n- Work with the dimensions and the positions of the elements\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe `beforeCreate` and `created` hooks are used for the early initialisation of a component\'s logic, when the DOM does not yet exist and you cannot work with it.","export default {\\n  created() {\\n    this.fetchData(); // the DOM is not yet available\\n  }\\n};"],[1365,"If you add a new property to an object after its initialisation, Vue will not make it reactive automatically. Such a property will not be tracked by the system of reactivity. As a result changes to that property will not cause an update of the template. This is connected with how Vue intercepts the access to properties. For correct working special methods have to be used.","Reactivity in Vue is founded on the tracking of the properties that exist at the moment of the component\'s initialisation.\\nWhy the problem arises\\nDuring the creation of the component Vue:\\n\\n- Passes over all of the object\'s properties\\n\\n- Makes them reactive\\n\\n- Remembers the dependencies\\n\\nIf a property is added later:\\n\\n- Vue does not know of its existence\\n\\n- Getters and setters are not created for it\\n\\n- Changes are not tracked\\n\\nAn example of the problem:\\n\\nThe consequences\\n\\n- The template is not updated\\n\\n- computed and watch do not react\\n\\n- The behaviour may look like a bug\\n\\nWhen this is especially critical\\n\\n- Work with forms\\n\\n- The dynamic extension of objects\\n\\n- The updating of data from an API\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe addition of new properties to an object after the initialisation does not make them reactive, so such changes do not lead to an update of the interface without special devices.","data() {\\n  return {\\n    user: {\\n      name: \'Alex\'\\n    }\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n// later\\nthis.user.age = 30; // not reactive"],[1366,"`computed` should be used for values that depend on reactive data and are used in the template. Methods are better suited for actions or computations that are carried out on an event. If you need a cacheable result, `computed` is chosen. If the logic has to be carried out every time, a method. This helps you avoid superfluous repaints.","The choice between `computed` and methods directly affects the readability of the code and the application\'s performance.\\nWhen to use computed\\n`computed` is suitable for derived data.\\nIt is used if:\\n\\n- The value depends on reactive state\\n\\n- The result is used in the template\\n\\n- Caching matters\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhen to use methods\\nMethods are suitable for the carrying out of logic.\\nThey are used if:\\n\\n- The computation is called on an event\\n\\n- Parameters are needed\\n\\n- Caching is not required\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe frequent mistake\\nTo use methods instead of `computed` in the template, which leads to superfluous computations on every render.\\nConclusion\\n`computed` is used for cacheable derived data, and methods for actions and computations started as they are needed.","<p>{{ filteredItems }}</p>\\n\\ncomputed: {\\n  filteredItems() {\\n    return this.items.filter(item => item.active);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nmethods: {\\n  handleClick() {\\n    this.logAction();\\n  }\\n}"],[1367,"If you do not clear the subscriptions and the events, a component may carry on working after its destruction. This leads to memory leaks and to unexpected behaviour of the application. Event handlers may be called for a component that no longer exists. Over time such errors accumulate. The cleanup of resources is therefore an obligatory part of the lifecycle.","Components in Vue often subscribe to external sources of events that live longer than the component itself.\\nWhat counts as subscriptions and events\\nSubscriptions may be:\\n\\n- handlers of the browser\'s events\\n\\n- timers and intervals\\n\\n- subscriptions to a store or an event bus\\n\\n- WebSocket and SSE connections\\n\\nWhat happens without the cleanup\\nIf the component is destroyed but the subscription remains:\\n\\n- Memory leaks\\n\\n- references to the component are preserved\\n\\n- the garbage collector cannot free the memory\\n\\n- Errors of execution\\n\\n- the code tries to update a destroyed component\\n\\n- errors of access to the state are possible\\n\\n- Unexpected behaviour\\n\\n- events are handled several times\\n\\n- the data is updated in \\"invisible\\" components\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nWithout the cleanup the handler will remain active even after the component is removed.\\nConclusion\\nThe cleanup of subscriptions and events prevents memory leaks and guarantees the correct behaviour of an application on a change of components.","mounted() {\\n  window.addEventListener(\'resize\', this.onResize);\\n}"],[1368,"You should unsubscribe from events before the destruction of the component. In Vue the `beforeUnmount` and `unmounted` hooks are used for that. In Vue 2 the analogues are `beforeDestroy` and `destroyed`. This guarantees that the component will no longer react to external events. Such an approach prevents errors and memory leaks.","The correct moment for the cleanup of resources is the stage of the component\'s destruction.\\nThe main hooks for unsubscribing\\n\\n- `beforeUnmount`\\n\\n- The component still exists\\n\\n- There is access to the state and the methods\\n\\n- The recommended hook for the cleanup\\n\\n- `unmounted`\\n\\n- The component has already been removed from the DOM\\n\\n- It is used more rarely\\n\\nFor Vue 2:\\n\\n- `beforeDestroy`\\n\\n- `destroyed`\\n\\nAn example of a correct unsubscribe\\n\\nThe special cases\\nWith the use of `keep-alive`:\\n\\n- `beforeUnmount` is not called\\n\\n- the logic of the cleanup is moved into `deactivated`\\n\\nConclusion\\nSubscriptions and events should be cleared in the hooks of the component\'s destruction, in order to avoid memory leaks and incorrect behaviour.","mounted() {\\n  window.addEventListener(\'resize\', this.onResize);\\n},\\nbeforeUnmount() {\\n  window.removeEventListener(\'resize\', this.onResize);\\n}"],[1369,"`package.json` describes the project and its dependencies. In it the names of libraries, the versions, the scripts and the project\'s metadata are held. This file is used by package managers for the installation of the dependencies. It determines how the project is built and run. Without it the standard work with npm or yarn is impossible.","`package.json` is the central configuration file of a JavaScript project.\\nThe main tasks of package.json\\nThe file carries out several roles at once.\\n\\n- The description of the project\\n\\n- the project\'s name and version\\n\\n- the description and the licence\\n\\n- The management of the dependencies\\n\\n- `dependencies`\\n\\n- `devDependencies`\\n\\n- the versions of the libraries used\\n\\n- The project\'s scripts\\n\\n- the commands of the build\\n\\n- the run of the application\\n\\n- the testing\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe interaction with the tools\\n`package.json` is used by:\\n\\n- npm, yarn, pnpm\\n\\n- bundlers and testing frameworks\\n\\n- CI/CD systems\\n\\nConclusion\\n`package.json` sets the project\'s structure, manages the dependencies and describes the ways of running and building it.","{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"my-app\\",\\n  \\"version\\": \\"1.0.0\\",\\n  \\"scripts\\": {\\n    \\"dev\\": \\"vite\\",\\n    \\"build\\": \\"vite build\\"\\n  }\\n}"],[1370,"`package.json` describes the dependencies and the permissible ranges of their versions. `package-lock.json` fixes the specific versions of the packages installed and their tree of dependencies. `package.json` is needed for the description of the project, and `package-lock.json` for reproducible installations. They complement one another. Together they ensure the stability of the environment.","In JavaScript projects two files are used for work with dependencies, each of which solves its own task.\\nThe purpose of package.json\\n`package.json` describes the project\'s requirements.\\nIt contains:\\n\\n- the list of the dependencies\\n\\n- the versions or the ranges of versions\\n\\n- the scripts and the project\'s metadata\\n\\nAn example of a dependency\'s version:\\n\\nThis means that updates within the bounds of the major version are permissible.\\nThe purpose of package-lock.json\\n`package-lock.json` fixes the result of the installation of the dependencies.\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- it contains the exact versions of the packages\\n\\n- it describes the complete tree of dependencies\\n\\n- it is generated automatically by npm\\n\\nThis file guarantees that:\\n\\n- every developer gets identical versions of the packages\\n\\n- the build in CI coincides with the local environment\\n\\nThe key difference\\n`package.json` answers the question:\\n\\n- what dependencies the project needs\\n\\n`package-lock.json` answers the question:\\n\\n- what versions are actually installed\\n\\nConclusion\\n`package.json` sets the rules, and `package-lock.json` fixes the specific state of the dependencies, ensuring the reproducibility of the build.","\\"react\\": \\"^18.2.0\\""],[1371,"The versions of dependencies are fixed by means of ranges of versions in `package.json` and a lock file. The ranges set the permissible updates, and the lock file fixes the exact versions. This lets you control the updates and preserve the stability. Such an approach is used in every modern project. It lowers the risk of breakages.","The fixing of the versions of dependencies is an important part of stable development.\\nThe ranges of versions in package.json\\nIn `package.json` the versions are specified by means of special characters.\\nThe main variants:\\n\\n- `1.2.3` — a strictly fixed version\\n\\n- `^1.2.3` — minor and patch updates are permitted\\n\\n- `~1.2.3` — only patch updates are permitted\\n\\n- `*` — any version is permitted\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe role of the lock file\\nThe lock file:\\n\\n- preserves the exact versions\\n\\n- fixes the nested dependencies\\n\\n- is used on every installation\\n\\nEven if the range permits an update, the lock file will not change without the explicit updating of the dependencies.\\nThe practical recommendations\\n\\n- Use a lock file and commit it to the repository\\n\\n- Update the dependencies knowingly\\n\\n- Avoid ranges that are too wide\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe versions of dependencies are fixed by a combination of ranges in `package.json` and exact values in the lock file, which ensures a balance of flexibility and stability.","\\"axios\\": \\"~1.6.0\\""],[1372,"A lock file guarantees that every developer will have identical versions of the dependencies. This prevents errors that arise only for part of the team. It makes the builds reproducible. A lock file especially matters in CI and in production. Without it it is hard to ensure stability.","In team development it matters that a project works identically for every participant.\\nThe problems without a lock file\\nWithout a lock file:\\n\\n- different developers may have different versions of the packages\\n\\n- errors are hard to reproduce\\n\\n- the build may differ in CI and locally\\n\\nWhat a lock file gives\\nA lock file ensures:\\n\\n- an identical tree of dependencies\\n\\n- predictable builds\\n\\n- stable behaviour of the application\\n\\nThe effect on CI/CD\\nIn CI:\\n\\n- the same versions are used as the developers have\\n\\n- the build becomes deterministic\\n\\n- the risk of \\"it works on my machine\\" is lowered\\n\\nThe practice in teams\\n\\n- the lock file is always held in the repository\\n\\n- changes in it go through code review\\n\\n- updates of the dependencies are carried out separately\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe use of a lock file is an obligatory condition of stable team development and of reliable builds.",null],[1373,"Modern IDEs have built-in tools for work with dependencies. They let you install, update and delete packages without the manual editing of files. IDEs also highlight outdated versions and conflicts. This simplifies the maintenance of a project. The work with dependencies becomes safer and clearer.","Modern IDEs substantially simplify the management of dependencies through their integration with package managers.\\nThe integration with package managers\\nIDEs work directly with:\\n\\n- npm\\n\\n- yarn\\n\\n- pnpm\\n\\nThrough the IDE\'s interface you can:\\n\\n- install a dependency\\n\\n- add a dev dependency\\n\\n- delete a package\\n\\n- update the versions\\n\\nThe hints and the analysis of the dependencies\\nIDEs provide additional capabilities.\\nThe main functions:\\n\\n- the highlighting of outdated packages\\n\\n- warnings about incompatible versions\\n\\n- autocompletion in the editing of `package.json`\\n\\nWork with lock files\\nIDEs:\\n\\n- correctly recognise `package-lock.json` or `yarn.lock`\\n\\n- show the changes in the tree of dependencies\\n\\n- simplify the code review of the changes\\n\\nThe integration with external tools\\nIDEs are often supplemented with:\\n\\n- a built-in terminal\\n\\n- plugins for the audit of dependencies\\n\\n- means of static analysis\\n\\nConclusion\\nModern IDEs provide convenient tools for the management of dependencies, lowering the quantity of errors and simplifying the support of projects.",null],[1374,"`key` is needed so that Vue can correctly match up the elements of a list on updates. It helps you determine which element has changed, been added or been deleted. Without a `key` Vue may reuse DOM nodes incorrectly. This leads to visual and logical bugs. A `key` is therefore obligatory for dynamic lists.","The `key` plays an important role in the algorithm of the updating of the DOM in Vue.\\nThe definition\\nA key is a unique identifier of an element of a list, which is used by Vue to track its state between renders.\\nHow Vue uses the key\\nOn the update of a list Vue:\\n\\n- compares the old and the new list\\n\\n- matches the elements up by their `key`\\n\\n- minimises the number of operations with the DOM\\n\\nWhat happens without a key\\nIf the `key` is absent:\\n\\n- Vue orients itself by the order of the elements\\n\\n- DOM nodes may be reused wrongly\\n\\n- bugs with inputs, focus and animations are possible\\n\\nThe requirements of a key\\nA good `key`:\\n\\n- is unique within the bounds of the list\\n\\n- is stable between renders\\n\\n- does not depend on the element\'s position\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nA `key` is necessary for the correct and predictable working of lists in Vue and should always be used.",null],[1375,"The use of the index as the `key` leads to the incorrect matching up of elements when the array changes. On the addition, the deletion or the sorting of elements Vue considers that the elements have simply \\"moved house\\". This can break the state of components, input fields and animations. The errors appear not at once but when the data changes. The index is therefore permissible only for static lists.","The problem of the use of the index as the `key` is connected with the fact that the index reflects the element\'s position rather than its identity.\\nThe main cause\\nA `key` has to identify an element of the data rather than its position in the array. The index changes on any operation with the list, even if the elements themselves have remained the same.\\nWhat happens when the list changes\\nWith the use of the index as the `key`:\\n\\n- Vue reuses the DOM nodes for the wrong data\\n\\n- the state of the child components \\"moves house\\"\\n\\n- the values of inputs may end up bound to other elements\\n\\nWhen the index is permissible\\nThe use of the index is possible if:\\n\\n- the list is completely static\\n\\n- elements are never added or deleted\\n\\n- the order of the elements does not change\\n\\nThe recommended alternative\\nIt is better to use:\\n\\n- a unique id from the data\\n\\n- a stable identifier not depending on the position\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nThe index as the `key` is dangerous for dynamic lists and can lead to bugs that are hard to catch.",null],[1376,"If you pass a string into `key` without the `:`, every element of the list will receive the same key. Vue will not be able to distinguish the elements from one another. This breaks the algorithm of the updating of the DOM. As a result visual and logical errors are possible. Such a `key` is in fact useless.","In Vue the presence of a `:` before `key` determines how the value is interpreted.\\nHow Vue reads the key\\nLet us compare two variants:\\n\\nIn the first case `key=\\"id\\"` is the ordinary string `\\"id\\"`.\\nIn the second the value is taken from the data.\\nThe consequences of a static key\\nIf every element has the same `key`:\\n\\n- Vue considers them one and the same element\\n\\n- the DOM nodes are reused incorrectly\\n\\n- the state of the components gets mixed up\\n\\nWhy the error is not always noticeable\\nThe problem may show itself only:\\n\\n- on a change of the order of the elements\\n\\n- on the addition or the deletion of elements\\n\\n- in work with forms and animations\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nA `key` without the `:` turns into a string literal and makes the list logically incorrect.","<li v-for=\\"item in items\\" key=\\"id\\"></li>\\n<li v-for=\\"item in items\\" :key=\\"item.id\\"></li>"],[1377,"The object notation of `:class` lets you tie the names of classes to conditions. A class is added if the corresponding expression is true. This makes the template clear and easily extensible. Such an approach is preferable to complex ternary expressions. It is widely used in real projects.","The object notation of `:class` is the most readable and safest way of conditional styling.\\nThe syntax\\nThe basic example looks like this:\\n\\nHow this works\\nVue:\\n\\n- takes the object\'s keys as the names of the classes\\n\\n- checks the values\\n\\n- adds only those classes where the value is `true`\\n\\nThe use with computed properties\\nFor complex conditions:\\n\\nThe advantages of the approach\\nThe object notation:\\n\\n- improves the readability\\n\\n- scales easily\\n\\n- simplifies the maintenance\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\n`:class` with the object notation is the standard and recommended way of managing CSS classes in Vue.","<div :class=\\"{ active: isActive, error: hasError }\\"></div>\\n\\ncomputed: {\\n  classes() {\\n    return {\\n      active: this.isActive,\\n      disabled: this.isDisabled\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n<div :class=\\"classes\\"></div>"],[1378,"`:style` lets you set inline styles on the basis of a component\'s data. The values can be computed dynamically through expressions or computed properties. This approach is useful for values that are hard to express through classes. It is not recommended for complex styling, however. Most often `:style` is applied for individual properties.","The `:style` directive is used when an element\'s appearance depends directly on the data.\\nThe definition\\n:style is Vue\'s mechanism for the dynamic setting of inline styles through a JavaScript object or an array of objects.\\nThe basic syntax\\nThe simplest example:\\n\\nVue will apply the corresponding value depending on the state of the data.\\nThe object form\\nThe most widespread variant:\\n\\nThe use of computed properties\\nFor more complex logic it is recommended:\\n\\nWhen to use it and when not\\n`:style` is appropriate if:\\n\\n- the value is computed dynamically\\n\\n- the style depends on the data\\n\\n- it is impossible to express it through classes\\n\\nFor complex visual states it is better to use CSS classes.\\nA brief conclusion\\n`:style` is suitable for pinpoint dynamic styling, but it does not replace full work with CSS.","<div :style=\\"{ color: isError ? \'red\' : \'black\' }\\"></div>\\n\\n<div :style=\\"{ width: size + \'px\', opacity: isVisible ? 1 : 0.5 }\\"></div>\\n\\ncomputed: {\\n  styles() {\\n    return {\\n      backgroundColor: this.isActive ? \'green\' : \'gray\'\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n<div :style=\\"styles\\"></div>"],[1379,"In JavaScript the absence of a value can be expressed in different ways. `null` and `undefined` mean the absence of a value, while an empty string is a valid but empty value. The check depends on the business logic. There is no universal check. You have to take the cases you need explicitly into account.","The correct check for the absence of a value depends on which states are permissible in the application\'s logic.\\nThe different kinds of \\"absence\\"\\nIn JavaScript there are several variants:\\n\\n- `undefined` — the value is not set\\n\\n- `null` — the value is deliberately absent\\n\\n- `\\"\\"` — an empty string, but there is a value\\n\\nThe explicit checks\\nWhat is most often used:\\n\\nThe check for null and undefined\\nTo check both cases:\\n\\nThis is a correct exception, since `== null` checks only `null` and `undefined`.\\nWhen falsiness should not be used\\nThe check:\\n\\nwill also fire for `0`, `false` and `NaN`, which is often undesirable.\\nThe practical approach\\nIt is recommended:\\n\\n- to check the states you need explicitly\\n\\n- not to mix the different kinds of absence\\n\\n- to take the business sense of the value into account\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nThe absence of a value in JavaScript has several forms, and a correct check has to take the specific context into account.","value === null\\nvalue === undefined\\nvalue === \'\'\\n\\nvalue == null\\n\\nif (!value)"],[1380,"A falsiness check counts several values as false. Strict comparison checks a specific value and type. A falsiness check is simpler but less precise. Strict comparison is safer in business logic. The choice depends on the task.","The difference between a falsiness check and strict comparison is connected with the rules of the coercion of types in JavaScript.\\nWhat falsy is\\nThe falsy values include:\\n\\n- `false`\\n\\n- `0`\\n\\n- `\\"\\"`\\n\\n- `null`\\n\\n- `undefined`\\n\\n- `NaN`\\n\\nThe check:\\n\\nwill fire for all of those cases.\\nStrict comparison\\nStrict comparison:\\n\\nchecks:\\n\\n- the specific value\\n\\n- the specific type\\n\\nThe risks of falsiness checks\\nFalsiness checks:\\n\\n- can hide errors\\n\\n- break the logic with permissible values\\n\\n- complicate the debugging\\n\\nWhen to use which\\nA falsiness check is appropriate:\\n\\n- in simple UI checks\\n\\n- when every falsy value is equivalent\\n\\nStrict comparison is better:\\n\\n- in business logic\\n\\n- in the validation of data\\n\\n- in work with forms\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nFalsiness checks are simpler but less reliable, whereas strict comparisons give a predictable result.","if (!value) {}\\n\\nvalue === null"],[1381,"Fallback content is displayed when the main data is absent or has not yet loaded. Conditional rendering is usually used. The UI shows a stub, some text or an alternative component. This improves the UX and prevents errors. Such an approach is used in practically every front-end application.","The display of fallback content is standard practice for robust interfaces.\\nThe definition\\nFallback content is alternative content that is shown to the user when the main data is absent.\\nThe basic approach\\nConditional rendering is most often used:\\n\\nThe widespread variants of fallback\\nIn real projects what is applied is:\\n\\n- textual messages\\n\\n- skeleton stubs\\n\\n- empty states\\n\\n- alternative components\\n\\nWhere the logic should be\\nIt is recommended:\\n\\n- to move the checks out into computed properties\\n\\n- not to complicate the template\\n\\n- to separate the logic of the data and the display\\n\\nThe practical effect\\nFallback content:\\n\\n- prevents errors of the render\\n\\n- makes the interface more comprehensible\\n\\n- improves the user experience\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nFallback content is an obligatory element of a reliable UI and has to be handled explicitly.","<div v-if=\\"items && items.length\\">\\n  <!-- the main content -->\\n</div>\\n<div v-else>\\n  No data\\n</div>"],[1382,"Semantic versioning is a scheme of versions of the form MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH. MAJOR is incremented on incompatible changes, MINOR on the addition of functionality, PATCH on the fixing of bugs. Such a scheme lets you understand which changes are safe for the updating of dependencies.","Semantic versioning is the standard for the naming of the versions of packages, which describes the nature of the changes in the code.\\nThe format of a version\\nThe definition:\\nA version has the form\\n`MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`\\nWhat every part means\\n\\n- MAJOR\\nIncompatible changes of the API\\n\\n- MINOR\\nNew functionality without a violation of the compatibility\\n\\n- PATCH\\nThe fixing of bugs\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nHow it is used in practice\\nIn the package.json you can specify ranges:\\n\\nThis means:\\n\\n- MINOR and PATCH updates are permitted\\n\\n- MAJOR ones are forbidden\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- The management of dependencies\\n\\n- The predictability of updates\\n\\n- Control of the compatibility\\n\\nConclusion\\nSemVer simplifies the management of dependencies and lets you update libraries safely.","1.4.2 -> 1.5.0  (a new capability has been added)\\n1.5.0 -> 2.0.0  (the old API has been broken)\\n\\n\\"react\\": \\"^18.2.0\\""],[1383,"`peerDependencies` are dependencies that a library expects to receive from the project rather than installing itself. They are usually used for libraries working on top of another library — React plugins, for example. This helps you avoid the installation of several versions of one and the same dependency.","`peerDependencies` are a way of specifying that a library has to work with a dependency already installed in the project.\\nWhat this is for\\nThe main task is to avoid the situation in which:\\n\\n- the library uses one version of React\\n\\n- the application another\\n\\nThat can lead to errors — to a disruption of the working of hooks, for example.\\nHow this looks\\n\\nThis means:\\n\\n- the library requires React\\n\\n- but it does not install it automatically\\n\\nWhen it is used\\n\\n- UI libraries\\n\\n- plugins\\n\\n- middleware\\n\\nThe difference from dependencies\\n\\n- `dependencies` are installed automatically\\n\\n- `peerDependencies` are not; the developer has to install them\\n\\nConclusion\\n`peerDependencies` are applied when a library has to use one and the same version of a dependency together with the application.","{\\n  \\"peerDependencies\\": {\\n    \\"react\\": \\"^18.0.0\\"\\n  }\\n}"],[1384,"In practice arrow functions, destructuring, spread/rest, optional chaining, nullish coalescing and async/await are often used. These capabilities simplify code and make it shorter and more readable. Most modern browsers and bundlers support them.","The modern standards of JavaScript (ES6+) added a multitude of capabilities that are actively applied in front-end development.\\nThe most used capabilities\\n\\n- Arrow functions\\n\\nThey are used for a concise syntax and for the preservation of `this`.\\n\\n- Destructuring\\n\\nIt simplifies the access to data.\\n\\n- Spread and Rest\\n\\nThey are used for the copying and the combining of data.\\n\\n- Optional chaining\\n\\nIt lets you safely turn to nested properties.\\n\\n- Async/await\\n\\nIt simplifies the work with asynchronous code.\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- The code becomes shorter\\n\\n- There are fewer errors\\n\\n- The readability is better\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe modern capabilities of JavaScript are used everywhere, since they simplify the syntax and raise the readability of code.","const sum = (a, b) => a + b;\\n\\nconst { name } = user;\\n\\nconst newArr = [...arr];\\n\\nuser?.profile?.email\\n\\nconst data = await fetch(url);"],[1385,"Events in the DOM pass through three phases: capturing, target and bubbling. In the capturing phase the event first passes from the top down through the DOM tree. For a handler to fire in that phase you have to pass the parameter `capture: true` into `addEventListener`.","The system of events in a browser is founded on the propagation of the event through the DOM tree.\\nThe phases of an event\\n\\n- The capturing phase\\nThe event goes from `window` to the target element.\\n\\n- The target phase\\nThe handler of the target element fires.\\n\\n- The bubbling phase\\nThe event rises back up.\\n\\nHow to subscribe to the capturing\\n\\nWhen capturing is used\\n\\n- global handlers\\n\\n- the interception of events before the bubbling\\n\\n- complex UI components\\n\\nWhy bubbling is used more often\\n\\n- the logic is simpler\\n\\n- there is less unexpected behaviour\\n\\nConclusion\\nCapturing is the first phase of the propagation of an event, which is used more rarely but is useful for the interception of events before they are handled by other elements.","element.addEventListener(\\"click\\", handler, { capture: true });"],[1386,"The subscription to events is carried out through `addEventListener`. The type of the event, the handler and an object of parameters are passed into it. You can specify the parameters `capture`, `once`, `passive` and others, which govern the handler\'s behaviour.","In a browser events are handled through a system of event listeners.\\nThe basic syntax\\n\\nThe main parameters\\n\\n- capture\\nThe handler fires in the capturing phase.\\n\\n- once\\nThe handler will be carried out once.\\n\\n- passive\\nIt tells the browser that the handler will not call `preventDefault()`.\\n\\nThis matters for performance — on scroll, for example.\\nUnsubscribing from an event\\n\\nWhen it matters to use the parameters\\n\\n- scroll and touch events\\n\\n- the optimisation of performance\\n\\n- the prevention of memory leaks\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe flexibility of `addEventListener` lets you control the behaviour of handlers and the performance precisely.","element.addEventListener(\\"click\\", handler);\\n\\n{ capture: true }\\n\\n{ once: true }\\n\\n{ passive: true }\\n\\nelement.removeEventListener(\\"click\\", handler);"],[1387,"The `sort()` method by default sorts elements as strings. Numbers are therefore compared lexicographically rather than by value. For example, `10` may end up before `2`. To sort numbers correctly you have to pass a comparator.","The `Array.sort()` method without arguments coerces the elements into strings and compares them character by character.\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nThe cause:\\n\\n- the strings `\\"10\\"` and `\\"2\\"` are compared\\n\\n- the character `\\"1\\"` is less than `\\"2\\"`\\n\\nThe correct sorting of numbers\\n\\nHow a comparator works\\n\\n- it returns a negative number — `a` comes before `b`\\n\\n- a positive one — `b` comes before `a`\\n\\n- 0 — the order does not change\\n\\nWhen this matters\\n\\n- the sorting of numbers\\n\\n- the sorting of dates\\n\\n- complex structures\\n\\nConclusion\\nWithout a comparator `sort()` works as a string sort, so for numbers a comparison function always has to be passed.","[1, 2, 10].sort();\\n// the result: [1, 10, 2]\\n\\n[1, 2, 10].sort((a, b) => a - b);"],[1388,"The simplest algorithm for the compression of a string is run-length encoding, in which identical characters are replaced with the character and the number of repetitions. For example, `aaabb` turns into `a3b2`. Such an algorithm is carried out in O(n), since the string is passed over once.","One of the simplest ways of compressing a string is the RLE (Run-Length Encoding) algorithm.\\nThe idea of the algorithm\\nThe definition:\\nRLE is a method of compression in which sequences of identical characters are replaced with the character and the number of repetitions.\\nAn example\\n\\nAn example of the implementation\\n\\nThe assessment of the complexity\\n\\n- The time complexity: O(n)\\n\\n- The space complexity: O(n)\\n\\nThe cause:\\n\\n- the string is passed over once\\n\\n- the result is held separately\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- simple formats of compression\\n\\n- the encoding of data\\n\\n- the handling of logs\\n\\nConclusion\\nRLE is a simple algorithm of compression, which is convenient for tasks with repeating characters and demonstrates the assessment of complexity well.","aaabcccc -> a3b1c4\\n\\nfunction compress(str) {\\n  let result = \\"\\";\\n  let count = 1;\\n\\n  for (let i = 1; i <= str.length; i++) {\\n    if (str[i] === str[i - 1]) {\\n      count++;\\n    } else {\\n      result += str[i - 1] + count;\\n      count = 1;\\n    }\\n  }\\n  return result;\\n}"],[1389,"Big-O is a way of assessing how the time of the execution or the consumption of memory of an algorithm grows as the input data increases. For example, O(n) means linear growth and O(n²) quadratic. This lets you compare algorithms independently of specific hardware or a specific language.","Big-O is used for the assessment of the efficiency of algorithms, in order to understand how they will work on large volumes of data.\\nThe main idea\\nThe definition:\\nBig-O is an asymptotic assessment of the growth of the time or the memory of an algorithm as the size of the input data increases.\\nThe most widespread complexities\\n\\n- O(1) — constant\\nAccess to an array\'s element by its index.\\n\\n- O(n) — linear\\nOne pass over an array.\\n\\n- O(n²) — quadratic\\nTwo nested loops.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nWhy this matters\\n\\n- the choice of the algorithm\\n\\n- the optimisation of performance\\n\\n- the design of systems\\n\\nWhat Big-O does not take into account\\n\\n- the constants\\n\\n- the particulars of the implementation\\n\\n- the processor\'s cache\\n\\nConclusion\\nBig-O helps you forecast the scalability of algorithms and choose more efficient solutions.","for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {\\n  // an O(n) operation\\n}"],[1390,"A function declaration is declared through the `function` keyword and is hoisted. An arrow function is written through the arrow syntax and has no `this` of its own. An arrow function also cannot be used as a constructor and it has no `arguments`.","In JavaScript there are several ways of declaring functions, but what is most often compared is a function declaration and an arrow function.\\nA function declaration\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- it is hoisted\\n\\n- it has its own `this`\\n\\n- it can be used as a constructor\\n\\nAn arrow function\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- it has no `this` of its own\\n\\n- it is not hoisted as a declaration is\\n\\n- it cannot be used with `new`\\n\\nThe main difference of this\\n\\nAn arrow function takes `this` from the outer context.\\nWhen to use which\\n\\n- An arrow function — callbacks, handlers, functional components\\n\\n- A function declaration — methods, constructors, APIs\\n\\nConclusion\\nArrow functions are convenient for short functions and callbacks, and function declarations for declarations that require hoisting or a context of their own.","function sum(a, b) {\\n  return a + b;\\n}\\n\\nconst sum = (a, b) => a + b;\\n\\nconst obj = {\\n  value: 10,\\n  method() {\\n    setTimeout(() => {\\n      console.log(this.value);\\n    });\\n  }\\n};"],[1391,"An ordinary `<script>` tag blocks the parsing of the HTML until the file has loaded and been carried out. The `defer` attribute lets the parsing continue and the script be carried out after the DOM has been built. The `async` attribute loads and carries the script out independently of the DOM. The choice of the mode affects the speed of the loading and the order of the execution.","Script tags directly affect how the browser builds the DOM and carries out the JavaScript.\\n1) The behaviour of an ordinary script\\nWhen the browser meets:\\n\\nThe following takes place:\\n\\n- The stopping of the parsing of the HTML.\\n\\n- The loading of the file.\\n\\n- The execution of the script.\\n\\n- The continuation of the parsing.\\n\\nThis is called parser blocking.\\n2) The defer attribute\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- The HTML carries on being parsed.\\n\\n- The script is carried out after the DOM has been built.\\n\\n- The order of the execution is preserved.\\n\\nIt is used for:\\n\\n- the application\'s main code\\n\\n- the initialisation of the interface\\n\\n3) The async attribute\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- It is loaded in parallel.\\n\\n- It is carried out right after the loading.\\n\\n- The order of the execution is not guaranteed.\\n\\nIt is used for:\\n\\n- analytics\\n\\n- third-party widgets\\n\\n4) Why this matters\\nIncorrect attachment:\\n\\n- Increases the time to the first paint.\\n\\n- Slows the building of the DOM down.\\n\\n- Worsens the Core Web Vitals.\\n\\nConclusion: an ordinary script blocks the parsing of the HTML, defer postpones the execution, async is carried out independently and faster but without a guarantee of the order.","<script src=\\"app.js\\"><\/script>\\n\\n<script src=\\"app.js\\" defer><\/script>\\n\\n<script src=\\"analytics.js\\" async><\/script>"],[1392,"Tasks land in the microtask queue after the execution of a Promise, `queueMicrotask` or a `MutationObserver`. They are carried out right after the current call stack has finished and before the next macrotask. Microtasks therefore have a higher priority. This affects the order of the execution of code and the painting.","Microtasks are part of the mechanism of the event loop, which determines the order of the execution of asynchronous code.\\n1) When microtasks are created\\nMicrotasks are added on:\\n\\n- The execution of `Promise.then`, `catch` or `finally`.\\n\\n- The call of `queueMicrotask`.\\n\\n- The use of a `MutationObserver`.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n2) When they are carried out\\nThe definition: The microtask queue is a queue of tasks that is carried out right after the current call stack has finished but before the next macrotask.\\nThe order:\\n\\n- The current code is carried out.\\n\\n- Every microtask is carried out.\\n\\n- Then the next macrotask.\\n\\n3) The difference from a macrotask\\nMacrotasks:\\n\\n- setTimeout\\n\\n- setInterval\\n\\n- DOM events\\n\\nMicrotasks:\\n\\n- Promise\\n\\n- queueMicrotask\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe result:\\n\\n4) Why this matters for React and for the browser\\n\\n- Microtasks may be carried out before the painting.\\n\\n- A large quantity of microtasks can delay the repaint.\\n\\n- This affects the performance.\\n\\nConclusion: microtasks are added in the working of Promises and similar mechanisms and are carried out right after the current code, before the macrotasks.","Promise.resolve().then(() => {\\n  console.log(\\"microtask\\");\\n});\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\\"macrotask\\"));\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\\"microtask\\"));\\n\\nmicrotask\\nmacrotask"],[1393,"The variables inside a function are created at the stage of the preparation of the context of the execution, before the function\'s code is carried out. The JavaScript engine first creates the variables and the functions, and then carries the code out line by line. `var` and functions are thereby initialised differently from `let` and `const`. The latter are in the so-called temporal dead zone until the moment of the declaration.","The creation of variables takes place not at the moment of the execution of the line but earlier — at the creation of the function\'s context of execution.\\n1) The two phases of a function\'s execution\\nWhen a function is called, the engine passes through two stages:\\n\\n- The creation phase (the creation of the context):\\n\\n- the variables are created\\n\\n- the functions are created\\n\\n- the scope is formed\\n\\n- The execution phase:\\n\\n- the code is carried out line by line\\n\\n2) What happens with the different kinds of declaration\\nThe definition: Hoisting is the mechanism by which the declarations of variables and functions are handled before the code is carried out.\\nThe behaviour:\\n\\n- `var`\\n\\n- it is created and initialised with the value `undefined`\\n\\n- `let` and `const`\\n\\n- they are created but not initialised\\n\\n- access is forbidden before the line of the declaration (the TDZ)\\n\\n- a function declaration\\n\\n- it is created completely\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n3) The temporal dead zone\\nThe definition: The Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ) is the area of code from the beginning of the block to the declaration of a `let` or `const` variable, in which access to it is forbidden.\\nAn example:\\n\\n4) Why this matters\\n\\n- It explains the order of the execution of the code.\\n\\n- It helps you avoid errors with undefined.\\n\\n- It lets you design the scope correctly.\\n\\nConclusion: variables are created at the stage of the creation of the context of execution, but they are initialised differently depending on the kind of the declaration.","console.log(a); // undefined\\nvar a = 10;\\n\\nconsole.log(x); // ReferenceError\\nlet x = 5;"],[1394,"A pure function should depend only on its arguments and should not change the external state. If a function uses an outer variable, the result may change without a change of the arguments. This complicates the testing and makes the code less predictable. In the functional style people therefore try to avoid external dependencies.","The use of outer variables is connected with the concept of pure functions.\\n1) What a pure function is\\nThe definition: A pure function is a function that:\\n\\n- Returns the same result with the same arguments.\\n\\n- Has no side effects.\\n\\nAn example of a pure function:\\n\\n2) What happens with the use of outer variables\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe problems:\\n\\n- The result depends not only on the arguments.\\n\\n- The external state may change.\\n\\n- The behaviour becomes less predictable.\\n\\n3) Why this is bad in practice\\n\\n- It is harder to test.\\n\\n- It is harder to reuse.\\n\\n- The risk of hidden bugs is raised.\\n\\n4) When this is permissible\\nSometimes it is fine:\\n\\n- Configuration.\\n\\n- Constants.\\n\\n- Dependency injection.\\n\\nConclusion: outer variables make a function dependent on the context, so pure functions try to use only the arguments.","function sum(a, b) {\\n  return a + b;\\n}\\n\\nlet tax = 0.2;\\n\\nfunction calc(price) {\\n  return price * tax;\\n}"],[1395,"An incrementing function can be made through a closure, by preserving a variable in the outer function. The inner function will have access to that variable and will change it on every call. The value will be preserved between the calls. This is one of the classic examples of the use of closures.","Closures let a function remember the variables from an outer scope.\\n1) What a closure is\\nThe definition: A closure is a function that has access to the variables from an outer scope even after the execution of the outer function has finished.\\n2) The implementation of the increment\\nAn example:\\n\\n3) Why this works\\n\\n- The inner function holds a reference to the variable `count`.\\n\\n- The context is not destroyed while the reference exists.\\n\\n- The value is preserved between the calls.\\n\\n4) Where it is applied in practice\\n\\n- Counters.\\n\\n- Caching.\\n\\n- The encapsulation of state.\\n\\nConclusion: a closure lets you hold state inside a function without global variables and without the passing of parameters.","function createCounter() {\\n  let count = 0;\\n\\n  return function () {\\n    count++;\\n    return count;\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst counter = createCounter();\\n\\ncounter(); // 1\\ncounter(); // 2"],[1396,"`var` variables in the global scope become properties of `window`, while `let` and `const` do not. This is connected with the fact that `let` and `const` are created in the global lexical environment rather than as properties of an object. They are available in the global scope but not through `window`. This was done for safer and more predictable work with scope.","It matters to distinguish the global scope and the global object — they are not one and the same thing.\\n1) How var works\\nOn the declaration:\\n\\nThe following takes place:\\n\\n- The variable is added to the global scope.\\n\\n- It becomes a property of `window`.\\n\\nThe check:\\n\\n2) How let and const work\\nOn the declaration:\\n\\nThe following takes place:\\n\\n- The variables are created in the global lexical environment.\\n\\n- They are not added to the `window` object.\\n\\nThe check:\\n\\n3) Why it was made this way\\nThe main reasons:\\n\\n- To avoid the accidental pollution of the global object.\\n\\n- To improve the isolation of the code.\\n\\n- To make the behaviour more predictable.\\n\\n4) The practical conclusion\\n\\n- It is not worth relying on access to variables through `window`.\\n\\n- It is better to export values explicitly if they are needed globally.\\n\\nConclusion: `let` and `const` create variables in the global scope but not in the global object, so they do not appear in `window`.","var a = 10;\\n\\nconsole.log(window.a); // 10\\n\\nlet b = 20;\\nconst c = 30;\\n\\nconsole.log(window.b); // undefined"],[1397,"In JavaScript the value by a dynamic key is obtained through square brackets. Inside the brackets a variable containing the name of the property is passed. The dot notation is not suitable for that, because it works only with static names. Such an approach is often used in work with APIs and forms.","When the name of a property is unknown in advance and is held in a variable, access through square brackets is used.\\nThe definition:\\nA dynamic key is the name of an object\'s property that is held in a variable and determined during the program\'s execution.\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhy you cannot use the dot\\nThe dot notation does not use the variable\'s value but takes the text literally.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Work with forms, in which the name of the field comes dynamically.\\n\\n- The handling of an API\'s data.\\n\\n- Universal functions for work with objects.\\n\\nAn example of a function\\n\\nConclusion\\nAccess through square brackets is applied whenever the name of the property is determined during the program\'s execution.","const user = {\\n  name: \\"Ivan\\",\\n  age: 25\\n};\\n\\nconst key = \\"name\\";\\n\\nconsole.log(user[key]); // Ivan\\n\\nuser.key // it will search for the property \\"key\\"\\n\\nfunction getValue(obj, field) {\\n  return obj[field];\\n}"],[1398,"LocalStorage is the browser\'s built-in store for saving data in the key-value format. The data is preserved between reloads and the closing of the browser. The values are held only as strings. It is usually used for settings, tokens and small caches.","LocalStorage is part of the Web Storage API and is used for holding data on the client side.\\nThe definition:\\nLocalStorage is a mechanism for the permanent holding of data in a browser in the `key-value` format, available through the `localStorage` object.\\nThe main operations\\n\\nThe particulars\\n\\n- The data is preserved after a reload of the page.\\n\\n- Access is possible only within the bounds of one domain.\\n\\n- The values are held in string form.\\n\\nAn example of the holding of an object\\n\\nWhen it is used\\n\\n- The holding of authorisation tokens.\\n\\n- The saving of the user\'s settings.\\n\\n- The caching of simple data of the interface.\\n\\nConclusion\\nLocalStorage is suitable for holding small volumes of data that have to be preserved between the browser\'s sessions.","localStorage.setItem(\\"token\\", \\"123\\");\\nlocalStorage.getItem(\\"token\\");\\nlocalStorage.removeItem(\\"token\\");\\nlocalStorage.clear();\\n\\nconst user = { name: \\"Ivan\\" };\\n\\nlocalStorage.setItem(\\"user\\", JSON.stringify(user));\\n\\nconst parsed = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\\"user\\"));"],[1399,"The `map()` method creates a new array, applying a function to every element of the original array. The original array is not changed. The number of elements is preserved. The method is used for the transformation of data.","The `map()` method is one of the main tools for the handling of arrays in JavaScript.\\nThe definition:\\n`map()` is an array method that returns a new array formed from the results of the execution of a function for every element of the original array.\\nAn example\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\n- It iterates over every element of the array.\\n\\n- It calls the callback function.\\n\\n- It saves the result in a new array.\\n\\nAn example with objects\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- The preparation of data for a UI.\\n\\n- The forming of lists in React.\\n\\n- The transformation of an API\'s response.\\n\\nAn example in React\\n\\nConclusion\\n`map()` is used when you have to transform an array and obtain a new result without changing the original data.","const numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\n\\nconst doubled = numbers.map(n => n * 2);\\n\\nconsole.log(doubled); // [2, 4, 6]\\n\\nconst users = [\\n  { name: \\"Ivan\\" },\\n  { name: \\"Anna\\" }\\n];\\n\\nconst names = users.map(user => user.name);\\n\\nitems.map(item => (\\n  <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>\\n));"],[1400,"The `forEach()` method iterates over an array and carries out a function for every element, but it does not return a new array. To form a new array you have to create it beforehand and add the elements by hand. This makes the code more verbose than with the use of `map()`. `forEach()` is therefore usually applied for side actions rather than for the transformation of an array.","The `forEach()` method is used for the sequential execution of a function on every element of an array.\\nThe definition:\\n`forEach()` is an array method that carries out the function passed to it for every element but does not return a new array.\\nAn example of the creation of a new array\\n\\nHow it works\\n\\n- It iterates over the elements of the array in order.\\n\\n- It calls the callback function.\\n\\n- It does not form a resulting array automatically.\\n\\nWhen it is used\\n\\n- Logging and debugging.\\n\\n- The changing of outer variables.\\n\\n- The carrying out of side actions.\\n\\nConclusion\\n`forEach()` is suitable for actions on the elements of an array, but if a new array is required, `map()` is more often used.","const numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\nconst result = [];\\n\\nnumbers.forEach(n => {\\n  result.push(n * 2);\\n});\\n\\nconsole.log(result); // [2, 4, 6]"]]');export{e as default};